window.PLUMBLINE_DATA = {"project": "The Divine Plumbline", "projectKey": "divine-plumbline", "version": "1.5.0", "built": "2026-11-18 20:18", "builtISO": "2026-11-18T20:18:06+01:00", "parts": [{"id": "O.1", "title": "Overview — The Divine Plumbline in Five Days", "order": 0, "metadata": {"kind": "overview", "summarizes": "P.1 and D.1–D.5"}, "file": "o1-overview.md", "notes": [], "blocks": [{"ref": "O.1 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ddf39d3bff", "text": "Overview — The Divine Plumbline in Five Days"}, {"ref": "O.1 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "68d0462001", "text": "A short summary of the five-day Divine Plumbline week, drawn from the plan (P.1) and the day texts (D.1–D.5). Each day has two parts of about 1.5 hours: Part 1 in the morning, Part 2 in the afternoon. The questionnaire is filled in on Monday night; Wednesday and Thursday each take one wall in depth and end in one-on-one times; Friday is the landing. Personal story slides are listed, not summarized; \"not covered\" means the story is still a placeholder."}, {"ref": "O.1 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "faf4ce7100", "text": "Day 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline / Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "O.1 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "6407d1af46", "text": "Read Day 1 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "71ea33c5f0", "text": "Part 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline"}, {"ref": "O.1 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-2-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "d7e991db99", "text": "The week opens in prayer, with an honest, relational tone. Amos 7:7-9 introduces the plumbline: an absolute, unmoving standard. It is our behavior, not our personality, that is built like a wall, brick by protective brick; repeated long enough, it becomes a spiritual stronghold (2 Corinthians 10:4-5). The true plumbline to measure against is Christ. Then the four identity questions (Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology), the Belief Tree, a world views survey, and the biblical \"heart\" as the whole inner person (Proverbs 4:23)."}, {"ref": "O.1 §2 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-2-2", "kind": "list", "h": "6ae1bb4523", "text": "Exercise: Icebreaker \"Growing Up\" (Slide 5) Stories: Slides 1, 8 written (Review 1); personal, not summarized"}, {"ref": "O.1 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "12af2153a5", "text": "Part 2 — Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "O.1 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-3-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "ac7f25718a", "text": "Who has been telling us what to see: the seven false prophets (Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself). None is evil by definition, but each becomes a false prophet when it replaces God's voice on who we are. Satan as the ultimate deceiver (Amos 5:19) plays the long game of distorted identity. Storms of life do not create the wall; they reveal it. The day ends by handing out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False) to fill in that evening, unscored, then the Day 1 recap."}, {"ref": "O.1 §3 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-3-2", "kind": "list", "h": "2d50e28b0f", "text": "Exercise: \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\", quiet journaling (Slide 15) Stories (not covered): Slides 12, 13"}, {"ref": "O.1 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6238fbf296", "text": "Day 2 — Love Deficit & the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "O.1 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-4-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "178a1ad445", "text": "Read Day 2 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f7e69995f5", "text": "Part 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern"}, {"ref": "O.1 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-5-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "02f6cdfca1", "text": "When the need to be loved, valued and secure is not met, an identity crisis begins. The Love Deficit equation; visiting vs. blessing (Exodus 20:5-6), with sour grapes as its picture (Ezekiel 18:2); and the generational symptom chain from the book, where childhood reactions harden into adult symptoms. The same deficit leads down two roads: Rejection (\"I am not enough\") and Rebellion (\"I will make myself enough\"). Through Jesus' atonement we can break our agreement with what was spoken over us."}, {"ref": "O.1 §5 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-5-2", "kind": "list", "h": "5b3e609a45", "text": "Exercise: \"Where Do You Feel the Deficit?\", move to a corner of the room (Slide 5) Stories (not covered): Slides 3, 7"}, {"ref": "O.1 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "49d576f8b0", "text": "Part 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "O.1 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-6-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "321874ca50", "text": "Compact on purpose. The two walls at a glance: Rejection (cornerstone: unbelief) and Rebellion (cornerstone: pride), with a few recognizable bricks each. Everyone scores the questionnaire they filled in on Monday night, then the four typical situations are revealed: O Compliant (Saul), U Can't Do It (Moses), W Competitive (Jacob), C Critical (Judas), each with its core message and manipulator type. They are not mutually exclusive: a high score names a wall you built, not who you are."}, {"ref": "O.1 §6 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-6-2", "kind": "list", "h": "3d323ae5f8", "text": "Exercise: \"Both Walls\" diagram and scoring last night's questionnaire (Slide 13) Stories (not covered): Slide 12"}, {"ref": "O.1 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "be05b54576", "text": "Day 3 — The Wall of Rebellion: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) / Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "O.1 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-7-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "d589f5365f", "text": "Read Day 3 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a8130008cb", "text": "Part 1 — The Wall of Rebellion and the Pride Trap (Fight Swing)"}, {"ref": "O.1 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-8-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "e21701fd34", "text": "Rebellion in depth: the wall's cornerstone (pride) and full brick inventory, and pride as self-sufficiency, the trap that leaves people less secure the more they grasp. The Fight Swing moves between control/domination and collapse/burnout, and secular humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\" can quietly reinforce it. Then the Competitive (Jacob) and Critical (Judas) situations in depth, and the remedy: humility."}, {"ref": "O.1 §8 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-8-2", "kind": "list", "h": "25ac5b5b87", "text": "Exercise: \"Spot the Superman Message\", group activity (Slide 8) Stories (not covered): Slide 5"}, {"ref": "O.1 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "011e2160d9", "text": "Part 2 — Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "O.1 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-9-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "fddc91d424", "text": "Awareness is not enough; restoration is needed. The tone shifts from diagnosis to rebuilding: gold refined by fire and clay shaped by the Potter; God doesn't leave us to ourselves. An overview of the seven reinforcing rods, then Rod 1, Revelation (exposing to heal, not to shame), and Rod 2, Repentance (today with the weight on pride), closing with a written repentance sentence. Then the first one-on-one times: each student with one guide, in listening prayer, asking the Holy Spirit to show a brick and the lie behind it, and responding with the rods."}, {"ref": "O.1 §9 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-9-2", "kind": "list", "h": "9bede50491", "text": "Exercise: \"Naming One Brick\", private (Slide 17) Stories (not covered): Slide 16"}, {"ref": "O.1 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "68558001b6", "text": "Day 4 — The Wall of Rejection: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "O.1 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-10-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "e0d9f1b130", "text": "Read Day 4 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f569857abd", "text": "Part 1 — The Wall of Rejection, the Lust Trap (Flight Swing), and the Final Swing"}, {"ref": "O.1 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-11-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "f0cd6a01d8", "text": "Rejection in depth: the wall's cornerstone (unbelief) and full brick inventory, and the Compliant (Saul) and Can't Do It (Moses) situations. \"Lust\" in a broad sense, any craving used to numb or escape pain, deepens the wound it tries to escape; the Flight Swing moves between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal. Eating disorders as a case study (pastoral tone), Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory, and the remedy: God-declared worth. The Final Swing: left unaddressed, both swings can converge (a warning, not a diagnosis)."}, {"ref": "O.1 §11 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-11-2", "kind": "list", "h": "387aa26064", "text": "Exercise: \"Naming the Swing\", quiet reflection and stretch (Slide 6) Stories (not covered): Slide 5"}, {"ref": "O.1 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "83dc006337", "text": "Part 2 — Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "O.1 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1df9492561", "text": "Rod 3, Release: forgiveness as releasing a debt, a decision rather than a feeling; the book's study guide calls this session \"The Daily Cross\". Rods 4–5: recognizing the enemy's tactics and renouncing them with an active, spoken break, practiced first as a group with a light example. Rods 1–5 are tied together, and the second round of one-on-one times follows, now also with forgiveness and renouncing."}, {"ref": "O.1 §12 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-12-2", "kind": "list", "h": "6dc55ac800", "text": "Exercise: Rods 4–5 Recognize and Renounce, stand-and-move (Slide 16) Stories (not covered): Slide 15"}, {"ref": "O.1 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2c6c999c08", "text": "Day 5 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning"}, {"ref": "O.1 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-13-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "cf25ef77db", "text": "Read Day 5 in full"}, {"ref": "O.1 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "bacc67c9f0", "text": "Part 1 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind"}, {"ref": "O.1 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-14-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "4a8812b68c", "text": "The landing. The daily cross: the one sacrifice already made, that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles. It is the altar fulfilled: the Altar (Ezra 3) is the place you return to every time you sin, and the Temple is the ongoing life with God lived from there. Then Rod 6, Renewing (Romans 12:2), in eight steps: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping."}, {"ref": "O.1 §14 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-14-2", "kind": "list", "h": "bd185d9c6a", "text": "Exercise: \"Practice the A-C-D-B Loop\", together and then privately (Slide 9) Stories (not covered): Slides 6, 11"}, {"ref": "O.1 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-O.1-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "fedcbd5ce3", "text": "Part 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning"}, {"ref": "O.1 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-O.1-15-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "d3d7bc3608", "text": "Rod 7, Rebuilding: a new wall laid on the true plumbline (Christ), following Nehemiah after Ezra's altar and temple. The ten gates of Jerusalem as an allegory of restored life (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch), and the King of Glory (Psalm 24), with a quiet look back at Monday night's questionnaire. The week closes with a full recap, testimonies and encouragement, and a recommissioning prayer that sends everyone out with the daily cross."}, {"ref": "O.1 §15 ¶2", "anchor": "p-O.1-15-2", "kind": "list", "h": "249766e83b", "text": "Exercise: \"Match the Gate to Its Meaning\", team race (Slide 18) Stories (not covered): Slides 20, 23"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ddf39d3bff\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-O.1-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Overview — The Divine Plumbline in Five Days</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"68d0462001\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-O.1-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">A short summary of the five-day Divine Plumbline week, drawn from the plan (P.1) and the day texts (D.1–D.5). Each day has two parts of about 1.5 hours: Part 1 in the morning, Part 2 in the afternoon. The questionnaire is filled in on Monday night; Wednesday and Thursday each take one wall in depth and end in one-on-one times; Friday is the landing. Personal story slides are listed, not summarized; \"not covered\" means the story is still a placeholder.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"faf4ce7100\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-O.1-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline / Truth and Deception</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6407d1af46\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-O.1-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d1-day1.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.1\">Read Day 1 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"71ea33c5f0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-O.1-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d7e991db99\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-O.1-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">The week opens in prayer, with an honest, relational tone. Amos 7:7-9 introduces the plumbline: an absolute, unmoving standard. It is our behavior, not our personality, that is built like a wall, brick by protective brick; repeated long enough, it becomes a spiritual stronghold (2 Corinthians 10:4-5). The true plumbline to measure against is Christ. Then the four identity questions (Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology), the Belief Tree, a world views survey, and the biblical \"heart\" as the whole inner person (Proverbs 4:23).</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6ae1bb4523\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §2 ¶2\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-O.1-2-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §2 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §2 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> Icebreaker \"Growing Up\" (Slide 5)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories:</strong> Slides 1, 8 written (Review 1); personal, not summarized</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"12af2153a5\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-O.1-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Truth and Deception</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ac7f25718a\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-O.1-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Who has been telling us what to see: the seven false prophets (Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself). None is evil by definition, but each becomes a false prophet when it replaces God's voice on who we are. Satan as the ultimate deceiver (Amos 5:19) plays the long game of distorted identity. Storms of life do not create the wall; they reveal it. The day ends by handing out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False) to fill in that evening, unscored, then the Day 1 recap.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d50e28b0f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §3 ¶2\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-O.1-3-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §3 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §3 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\", quiet journaling (Slide 15)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 12, 13</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6238fbf296\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-O.1-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 2 — Love Deficit &amp; the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"178a1ad445\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-O.1-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d2-day2.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.2\">Read Day 2 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f7e69995f5\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-O.1-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"02f6cdfca1\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-O.1-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">When the need to be loved, valued and secure is not met, an identity crisis begins. The Love Deficit equation; visiting vs. blessing (Exodus 20:5-6), with sour grapes as its picture (Ezekiel 18:2); and the generational symptom chain from the book, where childhood reactions harden into adult symptoms. The same deficit leads down two roads: Rejection (\"I am not enough\") and Rebellion (\"I will make myself enough\"). Through Jesus' atonement we can break our agreement with what was spoken over us.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5b3e609a45\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §5 ¶2\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-O.1-5-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §5 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §5 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Where Do You Feel the Deficit?\", move to a corner of the room (Slide 5)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 3, 7</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"49d576f8b0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-O.1-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"321874ca50\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-O.1-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Compact on purpose. The two walls at a glance: Rejection (cornerstone: unbelief) and Rebellion (cornerstone: pride), with a few recognizable bricks each. Everyone scores the questionnaire they filled in on Monday night, then the four typical situations are revealed: O Compliant (Saul), U Can't Do It (Moses), W Competitive (Jacob), C Critical (Judas), each with its core message and manipulator type. They are not mutually exclusive: a high score names a wall you built, not who you are.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3d323ae5f8\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §6 ¶2\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-O.1-6-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §6 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §6 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Both Walls\" diagram and scoring last night's questionnaire (Slide 13)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 12</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"be05b54576\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-O.1-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 3 — The Wall of Rebellion: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) / Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d589f5365f\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-O.1-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d3-day3.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.3\">Read Day 3 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a8130008cb\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-O.1-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — The Wall of Rebellion and the Pride Trap (Fight Swing)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e21701fd34\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-O.1-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rebellion in depth: the wall's cornerstone (pride) and full brick inventory, and pride as self-sufficiency, the trap that leaves people less secure the more they grasp. The Fight Swing moves between control/domination and collapse/burnout, and secular humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\" can quietly reinforce it. Then the Competitive (Jacob) and Critical (Judas) situations in depth, and the remedy: humility.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"25ac5b5b87\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §8 ¶2\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-O.1-8-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §8 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §8 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Spot the Superman Message\", group activity (Slide 8)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 5</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"011e2160d9\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-O.1-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"fddc91d424\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-O.1-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Awareness is not enough; restoration is needed. The tone shifts from diagnosis to rebuilding: gold refined by fire and clay shaped by the Potter; God doesn't leave us to ourselves. An overview of the seven reinforcing rods, then Rod 1, Revelation (exposing to heal, not to shame), and Rod 2, Repentance (today with the weight on pride), closing with a written repentance sentence. Then the first one-on-one times: each student with one guide, in listening prayer, asking the Holy Spirit to show a brick and the lie behind it, and responding with the rods.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9bede50491\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §9 ¶2\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-O.1-9-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §9 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §9 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Naming One Brick\", private (Slide 17)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 16</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"68558001b6\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-O.1-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 4 — The Wall of Rejection: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e0d9f1b130\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-O.1-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d4-day4.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.4\">Read Day 4 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f569857abd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-O.1-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — The Wall of Rejection, the Lust Trap (Flight Swing), and the Final Swing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f0cd6a01d8\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-O.1-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rejection in depth: the wall's cornerstone (unbelief) and full brick inventory, and the Compliant (Saul) and Can't Do It (Moses) situations. \"Lust\" in a broad sense, any craving used to numb or escape pain, deepens the wound it tries to escape; the Flight Swing moves between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal. Eating disorders as a case study (pastoral tone), Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory, and the remedy: God-declared worth. The Final Swing: left unaddressed, both swings can converge (a warning, not a diagnosis).</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"387aa26064\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §11 ¶2\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-O.1-11-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §11 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §11 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Naming the Swing\", quiet reflection and stretch (Slide 6)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 5</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"83dc006337\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-O.1-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1df9492561\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-O.1-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rod 3, Release: forgiveness as releasing a debt, a decision rather than a feeling; the book's study guide calls this session \"The Daily Cross\". Rods 4–5: recognizing the enemy's tactics and renouncing them with an active, spoken break, practiced first as a group with a light example. Rods 1–5 are tied together, and the second round of one-on-one times follows, now also with forgiveness and renouncing.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6dc55ac800\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §12 ¶2\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-O.1-12-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §12 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §12 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> Rods 4–5 Recognize and Renounce, stand-and-move (Slide 16)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slide 15</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2c6c999c08\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-O.1-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Day 5 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cf25ef77db\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-O.1-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><a data-href=\"d5-day5.md\" data-internal=\"1\" href=\"#d-D.5\">Read Day 5 in full</a></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bacc67c9f0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-O.1-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 1 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4a8812b68c\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-O.1-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">The landing. The daily cross: the one sacrifice already made, that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles. It is the altar fulfilled: the Altar (Ezra 3) is the place you return to every time you sin, and the Temple is the ongoing life with God lived from there. Then Rod 6, Renewing (Romans 12:2), in eight steps: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bd185d9c6a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §14 ¶2\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-O.1-14-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §14 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §14 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Practice the A-C-D-B Loop\", together and then privately (Slide 9)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 6, 11</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"fedcbd5ce3\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"O.1 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-O.1-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Part 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d3d7bc3608\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"O.1 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-O.1-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Rod 7, Rebuilding: a new wall laid on the true plumbline (Christ), following Nehemiah after Ezra's altar and temple. The ten gates of Jerusalem as an allegory of restored life (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch), and the King of Glory (Psalm 24), with a quiet look back at Monday night's questionnaire. The week closes with a full recap, testimonies and encouragement, and a recommissioning prayer that sends everyone out with the daily cross.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"249766e83b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"O.1 §15 ¶2\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-O.1-15-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference O.1 §15 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"O.1 §15 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶2</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Exercise:</strong> \"Match the Gate to Its Meaning\", team race (Slide 18)</li>\n<li><strong>Stories (not covered):</strong> Slides 20, 23</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "P.1", "title": "The Divine Plumbline — 5-Day DTS Program", "order": 1, "metadata": {"source": "src/divine-plumbline-5day-program.md", "kind": "5-day plan"}, "file": "p1-5day-program.md", "notes": ["<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"Assumed standard DTS daily rhythm: worship/devotions → Lecture Block 1 → break → Lecture Block 2 → lunch → afternoon Personal Ministry Time (PMT) / homework / journaling. Adjust slot labels to your base's actual clock times.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q2, 2026-11-16): \"- Amos 7:7–9 — the plumbline vision; wall = personality\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Administer the questionnaire</strong> (24-item T/F instrument) before revealing the four situations, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- <strong>Administer the questionnaire</strong> (24-item T/F instrument) before revealing the four profiles, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"## DAY 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Profiles\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Lecture 2: Rejection and Rebellion — the full brick lists</strong>\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- <strong>Reveal: the Four Personality Profiles</strong>\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Each with core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator-type mapping\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>PMT / Journal prompt:</strong> Score your own questionnaire; mark every brick you personally identify with.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## DAY 3 — Behind the Walls\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Lecture 1: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)</strong>\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Lecture 2: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing</strong>\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>PMT / Small groups:</strong> Using your questionnaire result, identify whether your own pattern leans more toward flight (lust) or fight (pride) — discuss with a partner, not the whole group.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## DAY 4 — Restoration Begins\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Lecture 1: Foundations, Revelation, Repentance</strong>\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Emotions/Mind/Will soul-swings converging toward instability — \"double-mindedness\" (James 1:6–8)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Rod 3 — Release/Forgiveness: the unforgiving-servant parable; unforgiveness as \"tormentor\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>PMT / Ministry time:</strong> Personal prayer ministry offered — walking a student through forgiveness release for one specific offense, using the Day 2 brick list as a starting point.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## DAY 5 — Renewing, Rebuilding, Commissioning\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Lecture 1: Renewing — the eight steps</strong>\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- The Altar (confession/repentance/forgiveness) and the Temple (worship/intimacy) as the two foundations rebuilt first (Ezra 3)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- <strong>Closing session:</strong> testimonies, re-score or reflect on the questionnaire in light of the week, commissioning/prayer over each student\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- This compresses the source material's 8 study-guide sessions (15 book chapters) into 5 teaching days by pairing sessions 1–2, 3–4, and 5–6 into single days and giving 7–8 (the longest, most practical restoration content) two full days.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Questionnaire timing (Day 2, before the reveal) is deliberate so students self-report before knowing what the letters mean — reconsider if your base prefers to teach the framework first and self-assess after.\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "P.1 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "55cab92ba3", "text": "The Divine Plumbline — 5-Day DTS Program"}, {"ref": "P.1 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "ca3108eed8", "text": "Assumed standard DTS daily rhythm: worship/devotions → Lecture Block 1 → break → Lecture Block 2 → lunch → afternoon one-on-one times (Days 3–4) / homework / journaling. Adjust slot labels to your base's actual clock times."}, {"ref": "P.1 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "55ab2902b1", "text": "DAY 1 — Foundations: Identity and Deception"}, {"ref": "P.1 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-1-1", "kind": "list", "h": "8d9e08a52c", "text": "Lecture 1: The Divine Plumbline Amos 7:7–9 — the plumbline vision; wall = behavior (people may view it as personality) Four identity questions: Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology Survey of world views (why all but Monotheism drift from \"I AM\") \"Heart\" defined biblically = whole inner person (soul + spirit) Lecture 2: Truth and Deception The seven \"false prophets\": Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself Satan as ultimate deceiver — Amos 5:19 lion/bear/snake illustration Storms (Ezekiel 13:9–16) and the two ways a wall falls: clenched fist vs. broken/contrite heart Tonight: the questionnaire (24-item T/F instrument), handed out at the end of the day and filled in that evening — before the walls and the four situations are taught, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching PMT / Journal prompt: Which false prophets spoke into your own life? Recall one storm that exposed a wall."}, {"ref": "P.1 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "598e070ebd", "text": "DAY 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "P.1 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "2bd02287d3", "text": "Lecture 1: The Love Deficit and Sour Grapes Love-deficit equations (You − Love = 0; You + Lust = 0; You + Love = Joy/Peace) Sour-grapes symptom chain (rage→crime, denial→personality disorders, etc.) Three response patterns: passive, aggressive, passive/aggressive Lecture 2: Rejection and Rebellion — a compact summary Cornerstones: unbelief (rejection) / pride (rebellion) The two walls at a glance, with a few recognizable bricks each; the full brick lists come in depth on Day 3 (Rebellion) and Day 4 (Rejection) Score the questionnaire from Monday night, before revealing the four situations Reveal: the Four Typical Situations Compliant (Saul) / Can't Do It (Moses) — from Rejection Competitive (Jacob) / Critical (Judas) — from Rebellion Each in brief: core message and manipulator-type mapping; strengths and weaknesses in depth with their wall on Days 3–4 PMT / Journal prompt: Check your scored questionnaire; mark every brick you personally identify with."}, {"ref": "P.1 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f33b677d32", "text": "DAY 3 — Rebellion in Depth; Restoration Begins"}, {"ref": "P.1 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d398d6a6b8", "text": "Lecture 1: The Wall of Rebellion and the Pride Trap (Fight Swing) Wall of Rebellion bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → homicide Inferiority → fight for superiority; secular humanism's \"superman philosophy\" Competitive (Jacob) and Critical (Judas) in depth: core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator type The remedy for pride: humility Lecture 2: Restoration Begins — Revelation, Repentance Rod 1 — Revelation: knowing Father God rightly vs. through a distorted human-father lens Rod 2 — Repentance: unbelief under Rejection, pride under Rebellion; humility as pride's antidote One-on-one times: each student with one guide, working with the Holy Spirit through their own issues — listening prayer, asking Him to show a brick and the lie behind it, and responding with the relevant rods (today Revelation and Repentance)."}, {"ref": "P.1 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1558b40921", "text": "DAY 4 — Rejection in Depth; Release, Recognize, Renounce"}, {"ref": "P.1 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4a990022cd", "text": "Lecture 1: The Wall of Rejection and the Lust Trap (Flight Swing) Wall of Rejection bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → suicide Pain → pleasure → temporary relief → return to pain (the addictive cycle) Eating disorders and their common parental preconditions Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory (pleasure always incurs a slower opposing pain) Compliant (Saul) and Can't Do It (Moses) in depth: core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator type The remedy for unbelief: grasping one's God-declared worth The Final Swing: Emotions/Mind/Will soul-swings converging toward instability — \"double-mindedness\" (James 1:6–8) Lecture 2: Release, Recognize, Renounce Rod 3 — Release/Forgiveness: the unforgiving-servant parable; unforgiveness as \"tormentor\" (the study guide's session \"The Daily Cross\") Rods 4–5 — Recognizing Satan's tactics (anger/fear, mocking, confusion, false friends, demonic influence) and renouncing them; the four weapons (name/blood of Jesus, testimony, armor of God) One-on-one times: the same way as Day 3, one guide per student, now also walking through forgiveness release for one specific offense and recognizing/renouncing, using the brick lists (Days 2–4) as a starting point."}, {"ref": "P.1 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "29bd3a5101", "text": "DAY 5 — The Daily Cross, Renewing, Rebuilding, Recommissioning"}, {"ref": "P.1 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6ae7451502", "text": "Lecture 1: The Daily Cross; Renewing — the eight steps The daily cross: the altar fulfilled — the one sacrifice already made, that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles (links back to Rod 3, the study guide's \"The Daily Cross\") The Altar (confession/repentance/forgiveness) — the place you return to every time you sin — and the Temple (worship/intimacy), the ongoing life with God lived from there (Ezra 3) Acknowledging → Believing → Confessing → Desiring → Exercising (A-C-D-B model) → Forgiving → Growing → Helping Practical exercise: walk through the eight steps against one identified wound from the week Lecture 2: Rebuilding and the Gates The nine/ten gates of Jerusalem allegorized (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch) Closing image: the King of Glory's character (Galatians 5:22–23 fruit) as the \"different character\" that emerges Closing session: testimonies, re-score or reflect on the questionnaire in light of the week, recommissioning/prayer over each student"}, {"ref": "P.1 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-P.1-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "021e96526b", "text": "Notes on pacing"}, {"ref": "P.1 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-P.1-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9a09f165e0", "text": "This compresses the source material's 8 study-guide sessions (15 book chapters) into 5 teaching days: identity and deception on Day 1; the love deficit and a compact look at the walls and situations on Day 2; each wall in depth with its trap on Days 3 (Rebellion) and 4 (Rejection), alongside the first five rods and one-on-one times; the daily cross, Renewing and Rebuilding on Day 5. If your base's DTS runs a half-day-per-lecture-block format rather than two blocks per day, split each day above into two, giving this 8–10 days instead of 5 — the content maps cleanly either way. Questionnaire timing (Monday night, before the walls are taught) is deliberate so students self-report before knowing what the letters mean — reconsider if your base prefers to teach the framework first and self-assess after. One-on-one times (Days 3 and 4) take the afternoon slot of the old Personal Ministry Time: one guide per student, working with the Holy Spirit through the student's own issues."}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"55cab92ba3\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-P.1-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">The Divine Plumbline — 5-Day DTS Program</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ca3108eed8\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-P.1-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\">Assumed standard DTS daily rhythm: worship/devotions → Lecture Block 1 → break → Lecture Block 2 → lunch → afternoon one-on-one times (Days 3–4) / homework / journaling. Adjust slot labels to your base's actual clock times.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"55ab2902b1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-P.1-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 1 — Foundations: Identity and Deception</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8d9e08a52c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-P.1-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Divine Plumbline</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Amos 7:7–9 — the plumbline vision; wall = behavior (people may view it as personality)</li>\n<li>Four identity questions: Ontology, Epistemology, Teleology, Axiology</li>\n<li>Survey of world views (why all but Monotheism drift from \"I AM\")</li>\n<li>\"Heart\" defined biblically = whole inner person (soul + spirit)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Truth and Deception</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The seven \"false prophets\": Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself</li>\n<li>Satan as ultimate deceiver — Amos 5:19 lion/bear/snake illustration</li>\n<li>Storms (Ezekiel 13:9–16) and the two ways a wall falls: clenched fist vs. broken/contrite heart</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Tonight: the questionnaire</strong> (24-item T/F instrument), handed out at the end of the day and filled in that evening — before the walls and the four situations are taught, so scores aren't anchored by the teaching</li>\n<li><strong>PMT / Journal prompt:</strong> Which false prophets spoke into your own life? Recall one storm that exposed a wall.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"598e070ebd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-P.1-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2bd02287d3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-P.1-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Love Deficit and Sour Grapes</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Love-deficit equations (You − Love = 0; You + Lust = 0; You + Love = Joy/Peace)</li>\n<li>Sour-grapes symptom chain (rage→crime, denial→personality disorders, etc.)</li>\n<li>Three response patterns: passive, aggressive, passive/aggressive</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Rejection and Rebellion — a compact summary</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Cornerstones: unbelief (rejection) / pride (rebellion)</li>\n<li>The two walls at a glance, with a few recognizable bricks each; the full brick lists come in depth on Day 3 (Rebellion) and Day 4 (Rejection)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Score the questionnaire</strong> from Monday night, before revealing the four situations</li>\n<li><strong>Reveal: the Four Typical Situations</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Compliant (Saul) / Can't Do It (Moses) — from Rejection</li>\n<li>Competitive (Jacob) / Critical (Judas) — from Rebellion</li>\n<li>Each in brief: core message and manipulator-type mapping; strengths and weaknesses in depth with their wall on Days 3–4</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>PMT / Journal prompt:</strong> Check your scored questionnaire; mark every brick you personally identify with.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f33b677d32\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-P.1-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 3 — Rebellion in Depth; Restoration Begins</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d398d6a6b8\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-P.1-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Wall of Rebellion and the Pride Trap (Fight Swing)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Wall of Rebellion bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → homicide</li>\n<li>Inferiority → fight for superiority; secular humanism's \"superman philosophy\"</li>\n<li>Competitive (Jacob) and Critical (Judas) in depth: core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator type</li>\n<li>The remedy for pride: humility</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Restoration Begins — Revelation, Repentance</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Rod 1 — Revelation: knowing Father God rightly vs. through a distorted human-father lens</li>\n<li>Rod 2 — Repentance: unbelief under Rejection, pride under Rebellion; humility as pride's antidote</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>One-on-one times:</strong> each student with one guide, working with the Holy Spirit through their own issues — listening prayer, asking Him to show a brick and the lie behind it, and responding with the relevant rods (today Revelation and Repentance).</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1558b40921\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-P.1-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 4 — Rejection in Depth; Release, Recognize, Renounce</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4a990022cd\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-P.1-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Wall of Rejection and the Lust Trap (Flight Swing)</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Wall of Rejection bricks (Emotions/Intellect/Spirit) → suicide</li>\n<li>Pain → pleasure → temporary relief → return to pain (the addictive cycle)</li>\n<li>Eating disorders and their common parental preconditions</li>\n<li>Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory (pleasure always incurs a slower opposing pain)</li>\n<li>Compliant (Saul) and Can't Do It (Moses) in depth: core message, strengths, weaknesses, manipulator type</li>\n<li>The remedy for unbelief: grasping one's God-declared worth</li>\n<li>The Final Swing: Emotions/Mind/Will soul-swings converging toward instability — \"double-mindedness\" (James 1:6–8)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Release, Recognize, Renounce</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Rod 3 — Release/Forgiveness: the unforgiving-servant parable; unforgiveness as \"tormentor\" (the study guide's session \"The Daily Cross\")</li>\n<li>Rods 4–5 — Recognizing Satan's tactics (anger/fear, mocking, confusion, false friends, demonic influence) and renouncing them; the four weapons (name/blood of Jesus, testimony, armor of God)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>One-on-one times:</strong> the same way as Day 3, one guide per student, now also walking through forgiveness release for one specific offense and recognizing/renouncing, using the brick lists (Days 2–4) as a starting point.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"29bd3a5101\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-P.1-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">DAY 5 — The Daily Cross, Renewing, Rebuilding, Recommissioning</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6ae7451502\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-P.1-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><strong>Lecture 1: The Daily Cross; Renewing — the eight steps</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The daily cross: the altar fulfilled — the one sacrifice already made, that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles (links back to Rod 3, the study guide's \"The Daily Cross\")</li>\n<li>The Altar (confession/repentance/forgiveness) — the place you return to every time you sin — and the Temple (worship/intimacy), the ongoing life with God lived from there (Ezra 3)</li>\n<li>Acknowledging → Believing → Confessing → Desiring → Exercising (A-C-D-B model) → Forgiving → Growing → Helping</li>\n<li>Practical exercise: walk through the eight steps against one identified wound from the week</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Lecture 2: Rebuilding and the Gates</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The nine/ten gates of Jerusalem allegorized (Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch)</li>\n<li>Closing image: the King of Glory's character (Galatians 5:22–23 fruit) as the \"different character\" that emerges</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Closing session:</strong> testimonies, re-score or reflect on the questionnaire in light of the week, recommissioning/prayer over each student</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"021e96526b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"P.1 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-P.1-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">Notes on pacing</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9a09f165e0\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"P.1 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-P.1-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference P.1 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"P.1 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>This compresses the source material's 8 study-guide sessions (15 book chapters) into 5 teaching days: identity and deception on Day 1; the love deficit and a compact look at the walls and situations on Day 2; each wall in depth with its trap on Days 3 (Rebellion) and 4 (Rejection), alongside the first five rods and one-on-one times; the daily cross, Renewing and Rebuilding on Day 5.</li>\n<li>If your base's DTS runs a half-day-per-lecture-block format rather than two blocks per day, split each day above into two, giving this 8–10 days instead of 5 — the content maps cleanly either way.</li>\n<li>Questionnaire timing (Monday night, before the walls are taught) is deliberate so students self-report before knowing what the letters mean — reconsider if your base prefers to teach the framework first and self-assess after.</li>\n<li>One-on-one times (Days 3 and 4) take the afternoon slot of the old Personal Ministry Time: one guide per student, working with the Holy Spirit through the student's own issues.</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.1", "title": "Day 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline / Truth and Deception", "order": 2, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day1.md", "kind": "Day 1"}, "file": "d1-day1.md", "notes": ["<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) introduces the week and the plumbline metaphor through trust-building and the four identity questions. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers where the \"wall's blueprint\" comes from — the seven false prophets, Satan as deceiver, and the storms of life. This is the first day of the whole program: no previous day to recap.\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Review 1, 2026-11-17)</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- [story about xxx — an early failure or vulnerable moment of your own, to signal \"I'm not up here as someone with it all figured out\"]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Set relational tone: this week works best when people are honest, not performing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- This isn't therapy and it isn't a performance review — it's a framework for understanding yourself biblically\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Promise: practical, not just theoretical — tools you can use starting this week\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Same question asked of Dr. Bruce Thompson decades ago — sparked a lifetime study of walls and plumblines in Scripture\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Review 1, 2026-11-17)</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- The plumbline: an absolute, vertical, unmoving standard — not adjusted to fit the wall\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- But behavior shapes personality more than we'd like to admit: repeat a protective behavior long enough, and you become very much what you act like — the wall, left unexamined, starts to feel like \"just who I am.\" This is what Scripture calls a <strong>spiritual stronghold</strong> (2 Corinthians 10:4-5) — a fortified pattern that feels permanent, but isn't\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- Belief Tree: these four questions sit at the base of a larger picture — worldview and belief form the roots, values and principles grow from them, and behavior (our bricks) is simply the fruit. Get the roots wrong, and the fruit follows\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Review 1, 2026-11-17): \"- [story about xxx — a moment where you realized a behavior you struggled with was really a heart issue, not just a willpower issue]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what this session covers (and a chance to answer any plenum question that came up during the break) — no recap needed yet. Midpoint activity to break and refocus around Slide 15 (position 6 of 7 — after the halfway point, not before). Closes with the full Day 1 recap and time for questions.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 2): \"### Slide 16 — Taking the Questionnaire\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 2): \"- Explicitly note: scoring comes next, before any teaching on what the four types mean — so no one's score is \"coached\"\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 16 — Recap: Day 1 in Review + Questions\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Full recap of the day's arc: the plumbline as an absolute standard → behavior built like a wall → the four identity questions → the heart as the whole inner person → the seven false prophets that mis-shape it → storms that reveal what's already built\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we get specific — the actual bricks, and the two walls most of us end up building\"\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.1 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "faf4ce7100", "text": "Day 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline / Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "D.1 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1d4be2a38d", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) introduces the week and the plumbline metaphor through trust-building and the four identity questions. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers where the \"wall's blueprint\" comes from — the seven false prophets, Satan as deceiver, and the storms of life — and ends by handing out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire to fill in that evening. This is the first day of the whole program: no previous day to recap."}, {"ref": "D.1 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1c7b961ba0", "text": "PART 1 — Welcome & The Plumbline"}, {"ref": "D.1 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "09a28ef788", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens straight into content (no recap — nothing came before it). Midpoint activity to break and refocus at Slide 5. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no recap needed yet, and questions are held for the break, not this session."}, {"ref": "D.1 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "90995b05ad", "text": "Slide 1 — Welcome to the Week"}, {"ref": "D.1 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "06ee5dead9", "text": "Open in prayer Welcome and warm greeting; introduce yourself as the teacher, not just a lecturer Name the week's destination without giving it all away: \"by Friday, you'll understand why your heart reacts the way it does — and what to do about it\" This is both for you to help others, but first to help you. Story: my wife and I staffed a few DTSes where Bruce and Barbara Thompson taught this. The first time it was sooo obvious where I needed help. But also the last time, there were things that I needed help with. Even preparing this helps me surrender more. Set relational tone: this week works best when people are honest, not performing. If you put up a mask, you will get help for the mask. So let's pray God gives us an environment where we can trust each other. To even express if we have reservations or distrust."}, {"ref": "D.1 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "638750d740", "text": "Slide 2 — Why This Week Matters"}, {"ref": "D.1 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6d82be12f4", "text": "Short, honest framing: everyone in the room has built protective patterns over the years This isn't a therapy session and it isn't a performance review. It's a framework for understanding yourself biblically, and to help you work through the issues the Holy Spirit wants you to take up, together with Him Practical, not just theoretical. Tools you can use starting this week"}, {"ref": "D.1 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1628102de9", "text": "Slide 3 — What Do You See? (Amos 7:7-9)"}, {"ref": "D.1 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "eb791db2a1", "text": "Read the passage aloud: the Lord shows Amos a plumbline against a wall God's question to Amos: \"What do you see?\" Same question asked of Dr. Bruce Thompson decades ago. It sparked a lifetime study of walls and plumblines in Scripture. So: what do you see? We will get to that. The plumbline: an absolute, vertical, unmoving standard — not adjusted to fit the wall, but the wall is adjusted to it"}, {"ref": "D.1 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2020831c6d", "text": "Slide 4 — Behavior Is Built Like a Wall"}, {"ref": "D.1 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a79ffa5fdc", "text": "Important distinction, stated early and clearly: it's not our personality that's built like a wall — it's our behavior . Each brick is a protective response we build (or copy from watching others model it) to keep ourselves safe, best we could, at the time it was laid Some bricks are laid by us, many are laid by others (parents, teachers, culture, pain) But behavior shapes personality more than we'd like to admit: repeat a protective behavior long enough, and you become very much what you act like — the wall, left unexamined, starts to feel like \"just who I am.\" This is what Scripture calls a spiritual stronghold (2 Corinthians 10:4-5) — a fortified pattern that feels permanent (but, spoiler alert: it isn't) A plumbline has two uses: it's held against a wall while it's being built , to keep each new brick straight — and it's used afterward , to measure whether the wall actually is. Measured honestly, a crooked brick can be identified and replaced This week: identifying your bricks, and finding the true plumbline (Christ) to measure against — before behavior hardens into identity"}, {"ref": "D.1 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5568ec4267", "text": "Slide 5 — [exercise] Icebreaker: \"Growing Up\" — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.1 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "b22555c127", "text": "Positive-focused exercise — pair-share or small group, two simple questions from childhood: How did you keep warm on cold days growing up? What's one episode where you remember feeling loved? Warm, easy entry point — good memories, not wounds; a relational, story-sharing breather halfway through the session before the next, more conceptual stretch Debrief briefly as a large group: invite a couple of volunteers to share one answer each"}, {"ref": "D.1 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ec9a5b824f", "text": "Slide 6 — The Four Identity Questions"}, {"ref": "D.1 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "03d6e794ab", "text": "Ontology: Who am I? Epistemology: How do I know what I know? Teleology: Why am I here / what's my purpose? Axiology: What do I value? Every worldview — Christian or not — answers these four questions, whether consciously or not Belief Tree: these four questions sit at the base of a larger picture — worldview and belief form the soil and roots; values and principles grow from them; behavior (our bricks) is the fruit. Get the roots wrong, and the fruit follows"}, {"ref": "D.1 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4377408fd1", "text": "Slide 7 — World Views Survey"}, {"ref": "D.1 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6d4db4572d", "text": "Quick contrast: how different worldviews (naturalism, humanism, biblical Christianity, etc.) answer the four questions differently Point: our answers to these four questions shape everything else — including how our behavior-wall gets built To-do for later today (and worth raising in your one-on-one, if you have one): of the four identity questions, which one do you least know your own answer to?"}, {"ref": "D.1 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "426986aaaf", "text": "Slide 8 — What Is \"The Heart\"?"}, {"ref": "D.1 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "7d5ef91f4e", "text": "Biblical definition: not just emotions — the whole inner person (Hebrew leb/lebab , Greek kardia ) Includes mind, will, emotions, and spirit together \"Out of it flow the issues of life\" (Proverbs 4:23) — why we start here instead of with behavior Story: when Maria and I got married, we both brought our issues with us. One of mine was anger, which was when things were too much out of the spectrum of what I would expect. Yes, there were underlying reasons, such as my inability to express my thoughts, but my response was not good for my wife nor my children. I did not want them to walk around on eggshells around me. I am still learning and still taking this to God. He still is good, Maria is still working through this with me. But it starts with the realization and being honest."}, {"ref": "D.1 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5b71dc0594", "text": "Slide 9 — Closing: What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.1 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c59000e084", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap needed, this is the first session of the week: \"after the break, we look at where these behavior-patterns actually come from — the voices that shape us before we even know it\" Explicitly note: hold any questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.1 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.1 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6f12213113", "text": "PART 2 — Truth and Deception"}, {"ref": "D.1 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "f0adfd2225", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what this session covers (and a chance to answer any plenum question that came up during the break) — no recap needed yet. Midpoint activity to break and refocus around Slide 15 (position 6 of 8 — after the halfway point, not before). Closes with the questionnaire for tonight, then the full Day 1 recap and time for questions."}, {"ref": "D.1 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "17c3432426", "text": "Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.1 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "362bc8ce8d", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"this morning we asked what do you see — now let's ask who's been telling you what to see\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on No recap of Part 1 needed — we're continuing the same day"}, {"ref": "D.1 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a3ef2c4abf", "text": "Slide 11 — The Seven False Prophets"}, {"ref": "D.1 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-14-1", "kind": "list", "h": "b1cf435c28", "text": "Introduce the concept: voices that shape our sense of identity before we can evaluate them List the seven: Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself Key point: none of these are evil by definition — but each can become a \"false prophet\" when it replaces God's voice as the final word on who we are"}, {"ref": "D.1 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a302d8435b", "text": "Slide 12 — The False Prophets, One by One"}, {"ref": "D.1 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-15-1", "kind": "list", "h": "12598e5859", "text": "[story about xxx — a specific false prophet (parent, teacher, peer, etc.) that shaped your own self-image growing up, for better or worse] Parents, Teachers, Peers: a parent's unspoken disappointment, a teacher's offhand label, peer rejection or approval — ask the room (rhetorical, not forced sharing): which had the loudest voice in your life? Government, Media, the Church: identity defined by nation, class, achievement; manufactured images of worth, beauty, success; even the Church can become a false prophet when performance or legalism replaces grace as the measure of identity The Heart Itself: Jeremiah 17:9 — \"the heart is deceitful above all things.\" Our own self-talk and self-judgment can be the loudest false prophet of all — sets up the whole week's tension: we need an outside, unmoving plumbline, not our own shifting self-assessment"}, {"ref": "D.1 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4e21138cfd", "text": "Slide 13 — Satan as the Ultimate Deceiver"}, {"ref": "D.1 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "5eba9dc574", "text": "Amos 5:19 — the lion, the bear, the snake: escape one danger only to run into another Satan's strategy isn't usually a single dramatic attack — it's the long game of distorted identity Deception works best when it feels like our own conclusion, not an attack [story about xxx — a personal experience of recognizing a spiritual attack for what it was, and how you responded]"}, {"ref": "D.1 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "3e75dbded1", "text": "Slide 14 — The Storms of Life"}, {"ref": "D.1 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "657685908e", "text": "Storms (crisis, loss, failure, betrayal) don't create the wall — they reveal it What's already built into the heart determines how we respond when the storm hits Sets up Day 2: the actual bricks that make up these walls"}, {"ref": "D.1 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "cbf809e950", "text": "Slide 15 — [exercise] \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\" — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.1 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "01de1406ba", "text": "Optional quiet journaling prompt: think of a recent hard moment — what did your immediate, gut-level reaction reveal about what's already built inside? Not shared aloud unless someone volunteers — this is personal excavation, not a public exercise"}, {"ref": "D.1 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "14bf3f11cd", "text": "Slide 16 — Tonight: The Divine Plumbline Questionnaire"}, {"ref": "D.1 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "160bfc846a", "text": "Hand out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False) Fill it in this evening, on your own, and bring it tomorrow: this is your profile, a mirror, not a verdict Instructions: answer honestly and quickly — first instinct, not overthought Explicitly note: don't score it yet — we score it together tomorrow, before any teaching on what the four types mean, so no one's score is \"coached\" Why tonight: everyone answers before the walls are taught, so the teaching doesn't anchor the scores"}, {"ref": "D.1 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.1-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "3f887444a2", "text": "Slide 17 — Recap: Day 1 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.1 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.1-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "89780af558", "text": "Full recap of the day's arc: the plumbline as an absolute standard → behavior built like a wall → the four identity questions → the heart as the whole inner person → the seven false prophets that mis-shape it → storms that reveal what's already built → tonight's questionnaire Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we get specific — the actual bricks, and the two walls most of us end up building. Bring your filled-in questionnaire\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"faf4ce7100\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.1-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline / Truth and Deception</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1d4be2a38d\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.1-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) introduces the week and the plumbline metaphor through trust-building and the four identity questions. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers where the \"wall's blueprint\" comes from — the seven false prophets, Satan as deceiver, and the storms of life — and ends by handing out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire to fill in that evening. This is the first day of the whole program: no previous day to recap.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1c7b961ba0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.1-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"09a28ef788\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.1-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens straight into content (no recap — nothing came before it). Midpoint activity to break and refocus at Slide 5. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no recap needed yet, and questions are held for the break, not this session.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"90995b05ad\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.1-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Welcome to the Week</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"06ee5dead9\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.1-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Welcome and warm greeting; introduce yourself as the teacher, not just a lecturer</li>\n<li>Name the week's destination without giving it all away: \"by Friday, you'll understand why your heart reacts the way it does — and what to do about it\"</li>\n<li>This is both for you to help others, but first to help you.</li>\n<li>Story: my wife and I staffed a few DTSes where Bruce and Barbara Thompson taught this. The first time it was sooo obvious where I needed help. But also the last time, there were things that I needed help with. Even preparing this helps me surrender more.</li>\n<li>Set relational tone: this week works best when people are honest, not performing. If you put up a mask, you will get help for the mask. So let's pray God gives us an environment where we can trust each other. To even express if we have reservations or distrust.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"638750d740\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.1-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — Why This Week Matters</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6d82be12f4\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.1-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Short, honest framing: everyone in the room has built protective patterns over the years</li>\n<li>This isn't a therapy session and it isn't a performance review. It's a framework for understanding yourself biblically, and to help you work through the issues the Holy Spirit wants you to take up, together with Him</li>\n<li>Practical, not just theoretical. Tools you can use starting this week</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1628102de9\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.1-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — What Do You See? (Amos 7:7-9)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"eb791db2a1\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.1-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Read the passage aloud: the Lord shows Amos a plumbline against a wall</li>\n<li>God's question to Amos: \"What do you see?\"</li>\n<li>Same question asked of Dr. Bruce Thompson decades ago. It sparked a lifetime study of walls and plumblines in Scripture.</li>\n<li>So: what do you see? We will get to that.</li>\n<li>The plumbline: an absolute, vertical, unmoving standard — not adjusted to fit the wall, but the wall is adjusted to it</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2020831c6d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.1-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — Behavior Is Built Like a Wall</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a79ffa5fdc\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.1-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Important distinction, stated early and clearly: it's not our <strong>personality</strong> that's built like a wall — it's our <strong>behavior</strong>. Each brick is a protective response we build (or copy from watching others model it) to keep ourselves safe, best we could, at the time it was laid</li>\n<li>Some bricks are laid by us, many are laid by others (parents, teachers, culture, pain)</li>\n<li>But behavior shapes personality more than we'd like to admit: repeat a protective behavior long enough, and you become very much what you act like — the wall, left unexamined, starts to feel like \"just who I am.\" This is what Scripture calls a <strong>spiritual stronghold</strong> (2 Corinthians 10:4-5) — a fortified pattern that feels permanent (but, spoiler alert: it isn't)</li>\n<li>A plumbline has two uses: it's held against a wall <em>while it's being built</em>, to keep each new brick straight — and it's used <em>afterward</em>, to measure whether the wall actually is. Measured honestly, a crooked brick can be identified and replaced</li>\n<li>This week: identifying your bricks, and finding the true plumbline (Christ) to measure against — before behavior hardens into identity</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5568ec4267\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.1-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Icebreaker: \"Growing Up\" — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b22555c127\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.1-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Positive-focused exercise — pair-share or small group, two simple questions from childhood:\n<ol>\n<li>How did you keep warm on cold days growing up?</li>\n<li>What's one episode where you remember feeling loved?</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Warm, easy entry point — good memories, not wounds; a relational, story-sharing breather halfway through the session before the next, more conceptual stretch</li>\n<li>Debrief briefly as a large group: invite a couple of volunteers to share one answer each</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ec9a5b824f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.1-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — The Four Identity Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"03d6e794ab\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.1-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Ontology: Who am I?</li>\n<li>Epistemology: How do I know what I know?</li>\n<li>Teleology: Why am I here / what's my purpose?</li>\n<li>Axiology: What do I value?</li>\n<li>Every worldview — Christian or not — answers these four questions, whether consciously or not</li>\n<li>Belief Tree: these four questions sit at the base of a larger picture — worldview and belief form the soil and roots; values and principles grow from them; behavior (our bricks) is the fruit. Get the roots wrong, and the fruit follows</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4377408fd1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.1-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — World Views Survey</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6d4db4572d\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.1-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick contrast: how different worldviews (naturalism, humanism, biblical Christianity, etc.) answer the four questions differently</li>\n<li>Point: our answers to these four questions shape everything else — including how our behavior-wall gets built</li>\n<li><strong>To-do for later today</strong> (and worth raising in your one-on-one, if you have one): of the four identity questions, which one do you least know your own answer to?</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"426986aaaf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.1-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — What Is \"The Heart\"?</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7d5ef91f4e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.1-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Biblical definition: not just emotions — the whole inner person (Hebrew <em>leb/lebab</em>, Greek <em>kardia</em>)</li>\n<li>Includes mind, will, emotions, and spirit together</li>\n<li>\"Out of it flow the issues of life\" (Proverbs 4:23) — why we start here instead of with behavior</li>\n<li>Story: when Maria and I got married, we both brought our issues with us. One of mine was anger, which was when things were too much out of the spectrum of what I would expect. Yes, there were underlying reasons, such as my inability to express my thoughts, but my response was not good for my wife nor my children. I did not want them to walk around on eggshells around me. I am still learning and still taking this to God. He still is good, Maria is still working through this with me. But it starts with the realization and being honest.</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5b71dc0594\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.1-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — Closing: What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c59000e084\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.1-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap needed, this is the first session of the week: \"after the break, we look at where these behavior-patterns actually come from — the voices that shape us before we even know it\"</li>\n<li>Explicitly note: hold any questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.1-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6f12213113\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.1-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Truth and Deception</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f0adfd2225\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.1-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what this session covers (and a chance to answer any plenum question that came up during the break) — no recap needed yet. Midpoint activity to break and refocus around Slide 15 (position 6 of 8 — after the halfway point, not before). Closes with the questionnaire for tonight, then the full Day 1 recap and time for questions.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"17c3432426\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.1-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"362bc8ce8d\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.1-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"this morning we asked what do you see — now let's ask who's been telling you what to see\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n<li>No recap of Part 1 needed — we're continuing the same day</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a3ef2c4abf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.1-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — The Seven False Prophets</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b1cf435c28\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.1-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce the concept: voices that shape our sense of identity before we can evaluate them</li>\n<li>List the seven: Parents, Teachers, Peers, Government, Media, Church, the Heart itself</li>\n<li>Key point: none of these are evil by definition — but each can become a \"false prophet\" when it replaces God's voice as the final word on who we are</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a302d8435b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.1-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — The False Prophets, One by One</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"12598e5859\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.1-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a specific false prophet (parent, teacher, peer, etc.) that shaped your own self-image growing up, for better or worse]</span></li>\n<li>Parents, Teachers, Peers: a parent's unspoken disappointment, a teacher's offhand label, peer rejection or approval — ask the room (rhetorical, not forced sharing): which had the loudest voice in your life?</li>\n<li>Government, Media, the Church: identity defined by nation, class, achievement; manufactured images of worth, beauty, success; even the Church can become a false prophet when performance or legalism replaces grace as the measure of identity</li>\n<li>The Heart Itself: Jeremiah 17:9 — \"the heart is deceitful above all things.\" Our own self-talk and self-judgment can be the loudest false prophet of all — sets up the whole week's tension: we need an outside, unmoving plumbline, not our own shifting self-assessment</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4e21138cfd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.1-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — Satan as the Ultimate Deceiver</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5eba9dc574\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.1-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Amos 5:19 — the lion, the bear, the snake: escape one danger only to run into another</li>\n<li>Satan's strategy isn't usually a single dramatic attack — it's the long game of distorted identity</li>\n<li>Deception works best when it feels like our own conclusion, not an attack</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a personal experience of recognizing a spiritual attack for what it was, and how you responded]</span></li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3e75dbded1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.1-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — The Storms of Life</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"657685908e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.1-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Storms (crisis, loss, failure, betrayal) don't create the wall — they reveal it</li>\n<li>What's already built into the heart determines how we respond when the storm hits</li>\n<li>Sets up Day 2: the actual bricks that make up these walls</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cbf809e950\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.1-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> \"Which Storm Revealed Your Wall?\" — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"01de1406ba\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.1-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Optional quiet journaling prompt: think of a recent hard moment — what did your immediate, gut-level reaction reveal about what's already built inside?</li>\n<li>Not shared aloud unless someone volunteers — this is personal excavation, not a public exercise</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"14bf3f11cd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.1-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — Tonight: The Divine Plumbline Questionnaire</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"160bfc846a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.1-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Hand out the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire (24 items, True/False)</li>\n<li>Fill it in this evening, on your own, and bring it tomorrow: this is your profile, a mirror, not a verdict</li>\n<li>Instructions: answer honestly and quickly — first instinct, not overthought</li>\n<li>Explicitly note: don't score it yet — we score it together tomorrow, before any teaching on what the four types mean, so no one's score is \"coached\"</li>\n<li>Why tonight: everyone answers before the walls are taught, so the teaching doesn't anchor the scores</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3f887444a2\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.1 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.1-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — Recap: Day 1 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"89780af558\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.1 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.1-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.1 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.1 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap of the day's arc: the plumbline as an absolute standard → behavior built like a wall → the four identity questions → the heart as the whole inner person → the seven false prophets that mis-shape it → storms that reveal what's already built → tonight's questionnaire</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we get specific — the actual bricks, and the two walls most of us end up building. Bring your filled-in questionnaire\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.2", "title": "Day 2 — Love Deficit & the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations", "order": 3, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day2.md", "kind": "Day 2"}, "file": "d2-day2.md", "notes": ["<p>original title (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"Day 2 — Love Deficit &amp; the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Structures\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the full brick inventory for both walls, administers the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire, then reveals the four typical situations and their behaviors.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the full brick inventory for both walls, administers the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire, then reveals the four typical structures and their behaviours.\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the two walls in full detail, then (before revealing anything further) taking the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire so everyone has their own honest starting point\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"## PART 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Structures\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 17, the 8th of 12 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 2 recap and time for questions.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Set expectations: this session gets personal — questionnaire included\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>moved (Restructure, 2026-11-18): Day 2 Slides 11–14 (both walls and their full brick inventories) are condensed into Slide 11 here; the full slides moved to Day 3 Slide 2 (Rebellion) and Day 4 Slide 2 (Rejection). Slide 16 (Taking the Questionnaire) moved to Day 1 Slide 16 (Monday night).</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 15 — [story about xxx — a moment you clearly recognized one of these bricks operating in your own life, either wall]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 17 — [exercise] \"Both Walls\" Diagram Walkthrough &amp; Scoring — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Fold in scoring the questionnaire here too: count T's per letter (O, C, U, W), compare to each type's max, and give people a moment to total their own scores privately — a mirror, not a verdict\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 18 — Reveal: Four Typical Situations and Their Behaviors\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"### Slide 18 — Reveal: Four Typical Structures and Their Behaviours\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- Not personality: these are four typical wall structures, and the behaviours each one produces\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 19 — The Four Situations in Depth\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"### Slide 19 — The Four Structures in Depth\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- For each type: core message (\"I'm not enough,\" \"I have to perform,\" etc.), key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type (passive, indifferent, competitive, active)\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 20 — Not Mutually Exclusive — and Not Who You Are\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 21 — Recap: Day 2 in Review + Questions\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls' brick inventories → your own questionnaire score → the four typical situations, their behaviors and their biblical archetypes\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls' brick inventories → your own questionnaire score → the four typical structures, their behaviours and their biblical archetypes\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind the walls — what happens when these patterns go unaddressed\"\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.2 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6238fbf296", "text": "Day 2 — Love Deficit & the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "D.2 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "cd4260bb10", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) gives a compact look at both walls, scores the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire everyone filled in on Monday night, then reveals the four typical situations and their behaviors. Each wall gets its full, in-depth day later: Rebellion on Wednesday, Rejection on Thursday."}, {"ref": "D.2 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e0aa7cba5b", "text": "PART 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern"}, {"ref": "D.2 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "dcdb25c0b6", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 1 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.2 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.2 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f76552bbd7", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 1: the plumbline as God's unmoving standard; behavior built like a wall; the seven false prophets; storms reveal what's already built Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Check that everyone has last night's questionnaire with them, filled in but not yet scored Today's turn: \"now we look at exactly how the bricks get laid\""}, {"ref": "D.2 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4ff5506d9d", "text": "Slide 2 — Identity Crisis: Where It Starts"}, {"ref": "D.2 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "bafddf496b", "text": "Every human being is born with a need to be loved, valued, and secure When that need isn't met — or isn't met consistently — an identity crisis begins This isn't about blaming parents; it's about understanding the mechanism"}, {"ref": "D.2 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5a3f7e682e", "text": "Slide 3 — The Love Deficit Equation"}, {"ref": "D.2 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a8de07393b", "text": "Present the equation(s) from the book: unmet love/value/security needs → deficit → compensating behavior Explain simply: every person, at some level, is either resting in being loved or working to earn/prove it [story about xxx — a time you caught yourself \"performing\" for love or approval rather than resting in being loved]"}, {"ref": "D.2 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "9bdf5c833f", "text": "Slide 4 — The Principle: Visiting vs. Blessing (Exodus 20:5-6)"}, {"ref": "D.2 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "aaf5a16964", "text": "The law states the principle: God visits the iniquity of the fathers on the children, to the third and fourth generation (Exodus 20:5, 34:7; Deuteronomy 5:9) — this is where the concept of generational sin is rooted Unresolved pain and wrong patterns don't stay contained — they pass down But read the contrast in the same breath: steadfast love to thousands, to a thousand generations of those who love Him (Exodus 20:6; Deuteronomy 7:9). The blessing outreaches the visiting by far — that is what God is really saying The picture of how it passes down: sour grapes — \"The parents have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge\" (Ezekiel 18:2, Jeremiah 31:29). People consuming and accepting what is wrong, and the next generation tasting it Not fatalism — naming the pattern is the first step to breaking it, and the greater weight is on the blessing side"}, {"ref": "D.2 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "38278b886d", "text": "Slide 5 — [exercise] Where Do You Feel the Deficit? — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.2 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "bccfd5a1cf", "text": "Quick physical/movement version: have people stand and move to a corner of the room representing where they feel most \"unfinished\" — being loved, being valued, or being secure Good stretch point at the halfway mark — no need to explain individual choices, just notice the room is genuinely split"}, {"ref": "D.2 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c8627d014e", "text": "Slide 6 — The Generational Symptom Chain"}, {"ref": "D.2 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "2ecb9988a1", "text": "Explain: the book cites U.S. military research on children whose fathers were away on deployment — the unresolved reactions of a child calcify over time into recognizable adult symptoms. The childhood reaction is the seed, the adult symptom is the fruit Show the table (childhood reaction → adult symptom): Rage → Crime Denial & Fantasy → Personality disorders Reunion attempts → Possessiveness Guilt → Depression (turned inward) / Delinquency (turned outward) Fear → Neuroses Impulse changes → Psychosomatic disorders Regression → Psychoses Example, worked through one line: a boy whose father is repeatedly absent feels guilt (\"it must be my fault\"). Turned inward, that guilt becomes depression; turned outward, it becomes acting out and delinquency. Same childhood reaction, two directions — which sets up the two walls Same pattern at everyday scale: a child who never gets the attention they need keeps trying to \"reunite\" — and as an adult clings to partners and friends (possessiveness) Key point: this is the mechanism of the generational pattern — unresolved reactions don't stay put, they harden and get passed on. At any link in the chain, truth can interrupt the cycle"}, {"ref": "D.2 §7 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.2-7-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "966c97fa5d", "text": "Delivery note: the clinical terms (psychoses, personality disorders) are the book's research labels — present them as illustrations of how far unresolved reactions can travel, not as labels for anyone in the room."}, {"ref": "D.2 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f5df79a1b6", "text": "Slide 7 — [story about xxx — a generational pattern you noticed in your own family line, and a moment where it started to break]"}, {"ref": "D.2 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "367353b302", "text": "Slide 8 — Two Roads From the Same Deficit"}, {"ref": "D.2 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "cc1e93a953", "text": "Preview: the same unmet need can produce two very different walls Rejection: withdrawing, believing \"I am not enough\" Rebellion: asserting, believing \"I will make myself enough\" Both are the same wound, answered in opposite directions — sets up the rest of the day We reinforce what those around us say: we want to belong, and we suffer from confirmation bias. First we think about what people say, then we accept it, agree with it, believe it — and finally speak it out over ourselves But this can be broken: Jesus is the atonement for our sins, and that is the root that lets you and me break our agreement with what was said — and not pay the price for it \"Anything you say can be held against you in a court of law\" — not if I recant it. Jesus has already paid for it, so the accusation cannot stand It gets replaced with what God says about us — every day, every time Sets up Days 4–5: a place to bring our daily struggles, and the words to break anything anyone has spoken over us"}, {"ref": "D.2 §9 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.2-9-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "12f6d4dc83", "text": "Delivery note: the courtroom line works best with a light touch and a smile (\"Tell Jesus the charge still stands!\") — humor makes the truth land, not lighter."}, {"ref": "D.2 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d561b753cc", "text": "Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.2 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "582e709cc0", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, a compact look at the two walls, then (before revealing anything further) scoring last night's Divine Plumbline Questionnaire so everyone has their own honest starting point Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.2 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.2 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "33900d3339", "text": "PART 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations"}, {"ref": "D.2 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-12-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "f8f3204a65", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. Compact on purpose: the two walls at a glance, the questionnaire scored, the four situations named — each wall gets its own day later in the week. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 13, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 2 recap and time for questions."}, {"ref": "D.2 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "17c3432426", "text": "Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.2 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "fdf89a242a", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"we said the same wound can build two different walls — let's name them\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on Set expectations: this session gets personal — scoring your questionnaire included"}, {"ref": "D.2 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3f099b2f", "text": "Slide 11 — The Two Walls at a Glance"}, {"ref": "D.2 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-14-1", "kind": "list", "h": "bb02d81f9e", "text": "The Wall of Rejection — cornerstone: unbelief; terminus (end state if unaddressed): despair, ultimately suicide — said gently and pastorally, not alarmist The Wall of Rebellion — cornerstone: pride; terminus: hostility, ultimately homicide — said carefully and pastorally Both built from the same three brick categories: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit Point out a few bricks people will recognize immediately: Depression, Apathy, Inferiority (Rejection); Competitive, Controlling, Resentment, Criticism (Rebellion) Keep it compact: the full brick inventories come on Wednesday (Rebellion) and Thursday (Rejection) — this is a naming slide, not yet a diagnosis slide"}, {"ref": "D.2 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "66c302a868", "text": "Slide 12 — [story about xxx — a moment you clearly recognized one of these bricks operating in your own life, either wall]"}, {"ref": "D.2 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "779be810a8", "text": "Slide 13 — [exercise] \"Both Walls\" Diagram & Scoring Last Night's Questionnaire — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.2 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "efd040954a", "text": "Show the \"Both Walls\" pyramid/brick diagram from the book In small groups, have people identify (privately) which side of the diagram feels more familiar — Rejection or Rebellion — without yet naming a specific brick Debrief lightly as a large group: it's common to feel \"both,\" and that's normal — no one is purely one wall Score the questionnaire everyone filled in last night: count T's per letter (O, C, U, W), compare to each type's max, and give people a moment to total their own scores privately — a mirror, not a verdict Good movement/discussion break at the halfway point of this session"}, {"ref": "D.2 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b87426b927", "text": "Slide 14 — Reveal: Four Typical Situations and Their Behaviors"}, {"ref": "D.2 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "81db9059df", "text": "Not personality: these are four typical wall situations, and the behaviors each one produces Now unpack what O / C / U / W actually stand for: O — Compliant (Saul) U — Can't Do It (Moses) W — Competitive (Jacob) C — Critical (Judas) Each tied to a biblical figure who displayed that behavior pattern"}, {"ref": "D.2 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b42847aa2a", "text": "Slide 15 — The Four Situations at a Glance"}, {"ref": "D.2 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "fd0f577e0c", "text": "For each type, briefly: core message (\"I'm not enough,\" \"I have to perform,\" etc.) and manipulator-type (passive, indifferent, competitive, active) Strengths and weaknesses come in depth with their wall: Competitive and Critical on Wednesday, Compliant and Can't Do It on Thursday Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in"}, {"ref": "D.2 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "1e31708868", "text": "Slide 16 — Not Mutually Exclusive — and Not Who You Are"}, {"ref": "D.2 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "76d779bfc2", "text": "Thompson's own conclusion: most people carry elements of more than one type, and can shift over time (he describes his own shift from Compliant toward Competitive) Reassure the room: having a \"high score\" in more than one type is completely normal State it plainly: a high score names a wall you built to protect yourself , not your identity — it's easy, over time, to mistake the brick pattern for the self underneath it, but it isn't the self Quick show of hands (optional, low-pressure): who scored highest in more than one type? Normalize it visibly"}, {"ref": "D.2 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.2-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "528df34631", "text": "Slide 17 — Recap: Day 2 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.2 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.2-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "81031b6a88", "text": "Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls at a glance → your own questionnaire score → the four typical situations, their behaviors and their biblical archetypes Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind one wall in depth — Rebellion — and the restoration begins, with your first one-on-one time\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6238fbf296\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.2-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 2 — Love Deficit &amp; the Generational Pattern / The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cd4260bb10\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.2-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 1, then covers the Love Deficit equations and the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing, with sour grapes as its picture). Afternoon session (Part 2) gives a compact look at both walls, scores the Divine Plumbline Questionnaire everyone filled in on Monday night, then reveals the four typical situations and their behaviors. Each wall gets its full, in-depth day later: Rebellion on Wednesday, Rejection on Thursday.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e0aa7cba5b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.2-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — Love Deficit and the Generational Pattern</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dcdb25c0b6\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.2-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 1 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.2-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f76552bbd7\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.2-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 1: the plumbline as God's unmoving standard; behavior built like a wall; the seven false prophets; storms reveal what's already built</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Check that everyone has last night's questionnaire with them, filled in but not yet scored</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"now we look at exactly how the bricks get laid\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4ff5506d9d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.2-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — Identity Crisis: Where It Starts</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bafddf496b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.2-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Every human being is born with a need to be loved, valued, and secure</li>\n<li>When that need isn't met — or isn't met consistently — an identity crisis begins</li>\n<li>This isn't about blaming parents; it's about understanding the mechanism</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5a3f7e682e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.2-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — The Love Deficit Equation</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a8de07393b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.2-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Present the equation(s) from the book: unmet love/value/security needs → deficit → compensating behavior</li>\n<li>Explain simply: every person, at some level, is either resting in being loved or working to earn/prove it</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a time you caught yourself \"performing\" for love or approval rather than resting in being loved]</span></li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9bdf5c833f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.2-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — The Principle: Visiting vs. Blessing (Exodus 20:5-6)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"aaf5a16964\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.2-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The law states the principle: God visits the iniquity of the fathers on the children, to the third and fourth generation (Exodus 20:5, 34:7; Deuteronomy 5:9) — this is where the concept of generational sin is rooted</li>\n<li>Unresolved pain and wrong patterns don't stay contained — they pass down</li>\n<li>But read the contrast in the same breath: steadfast love to thousands, to a thousand generations of those who love Him (Exodus 20:6; Deuteronomy 7:9). The blessing outreaches the visiting by far — that is what God is really saying</li>\n<li>The picture of how it passes down: sour grapes — \"The parents have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge\" (Ezekiel 18:2, Jeremiah 31:29). People consuming and accepting what is wrong, and the next generation tasting it</li>\n<li>Not fatalism — naming the pattern is the first step to breaking it, and the greater weight is on the blessing side</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"38278b886d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.2-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Where Do You Feel the Deficit? — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bccfd5a1cf\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.2-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick physical/movement version: have people stand and move to a corner of the room representing where they feel most \"unfinished\" — being loved, being valued, or being secure</li>\n<li>Good stretch point at the halfway mark — no need to explain individual choices, just notice the room is genuinely split</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c8627d014e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.2-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — The Generational Symptom Chain</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2ecb9988a1\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.2-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain: the book cites U.S. military research on children whose fathers were away on deployment — the unresolved reactions of a child calcify over time into recognizable adult symptoms. The childhood reaction is the seed, the adult symptom is the fruit</li>\n<li>Show the table (childhood reaction → adult symptom):\n<ul>\n<li>Rage → Crime</li>\n<li>Denial &amp; Fantasy → Personality disorders</li>\n<li>Reunion attempts → Possessiveness</li>\n<li>Guilt → Depression (turned inward) / Delinquency (turned outward)</li>\n<li>Fear → Neuroses</li>\n<li>Impulse changes → Psychosomatic disorders</li>\n<li>Regression → Psychoses</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Example, worked through one line: a boy whose father is repeatedly absent feels guilt (\"it must be my fault\"). Turned inward, that guilt becomes depression; turned outward, it becomes acting out and delinquency. Same childhood reaction, two directions — which sets up the two walls</li>\n<li>Same pattern at everyday scale: a child who never gets the attention they need keeps trying to \"reunite\" — and as an adult clings to partners and friends (possessiveness)</li>\n<li>Key point: this is the mechanism of the generational pattern — unresolved reactions don't stay put, they harden and get passed on. At any link in the chain, truth can interrupt the cycle</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"966c97fa5d\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.2 §7 ¶2\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.2-7-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §7 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §7 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: the clinical terms (psychoses, personality disorders) are the book's research labels — present them as illustrations of how far unresolved reactions can travel, not as labels for anyone in the room.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f5df79a1b6\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.2-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a generational pattern you noticed in your own family line, and a moment where it started to break]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"367353b302\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.2-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Two Roads From the Same Deficit</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"cc1e93a953\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.2-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Preview: the same unmet need can produce two very different walls</li>\n<li>Rejection: withdrawing, believing \"I am not enough\"</li>\n<li>Rebellion: asserting, believing \"I will make myself enough\"</li>\n<li>Both are the same wound, answered in opposite directions — sets up the rest of the day</li>\n<li>We reinforce what those around us say: we want to belong, and we suffer from confirmation bias. First we think about what people say, then we accept it, agree with it, believe it — and finally speak it out over ourselves</li>\n<li>But this can be broken: Jesus is the atonement for our sins, and that is the root that lets you and me break our agreement with what was said — and not pay the price for it</li>\n<li>\"Anything you say can be held against you in a court of law\" — not if I recant it. Jesus has already paid for it, so the accusation cannot stand</li>\n<li>It gets replaced with what God says about us — every day, every time</li>\n<li>Sets up Days 4–5: a place to bring our daily struggles, and the words to break anything anyone has spoken over us</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"12f6d4dc83\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.2 §9 ¶2\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.2-9-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §9 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §9 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: the courtroom line works best with a light touch and a smile (\"Tell Jesus the charge still stands!\") — humor makes the truth land, not lighter.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d561b753cc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.2-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"582e709cc0\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.2-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, a compact look at the two walls, then (before revealing anything further) scoring last night's Divine Plumbline Questionnaire so everyone has their own honest starting point</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.2-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"33900d3339\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.2-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — The Two Walls and the Four Situations</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f8f3204a65\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.2-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. Compact on purpose: the two walls at a glance, the questionnaire scored, the four situations named — each wall gets its own day later in the week. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 13, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 2 recap and time for questions.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"17c3432426\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.2-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"fdf89a242a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.2-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"we said the same wound can build two different walls — let's name them\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n<li>Set expectations: this session gets personal — scoring your questionnaire included</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3f099b2f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.2-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — The Two Walls at a Glance</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bb02d81f9e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.2-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The Wall of Rejection — cornerstone: unbelief; terminus (end state if unaddressed): despair, ultimately suicide — said gently and pastorally, not alarmist</li>\n<li>The Wall of Rebellion — cornerstone: pride; terminus: hostility, ultimately homicide — said carefully and pastorally</li>\n<li>Both built from the same three brick categories: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit</li>\n<li>Point out a few bricks people will recognize immediately: Depression, Apathy, Inferiority (Rejection); Competitive, Controlling, Resentment, Criticism (Rebellion)</li>\n<li>Keep it compact: the full brick inventories come on Wednesday (Rebellion) and Thursday (Rejection) — this is a naming slide, not yet a diagnosis slide</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"66c302a868\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.2-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a moment you clearly recognized one of these bricks operating in your own life, either wall]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"779be810a8\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.2-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> \"Both Walls\" Diagram &amp; Scoring Last Night's Questionnaire — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"efd040954a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.2-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Show the \"Both Walls\" pyramid/brick diagram from the book</li>\n<li>In small groups, have people identify (privately) which side of the diagram feels more familiar — Rejection or Rebellion — without yet naming a specific brick</li>\n<li>Debrief lightly as a large group: it's common to feel \"both,\" and that's normal — no one is purely one wall</li>\n<li>Score the questionnaire everyone filled in last night: count T's per letter (O, C, U, W), compare to each type's max, and give people a moment to total their own scores privately — a mirror, not a verdict</li>\n<li>Good movement/discussion break at the halfway point of this session</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b87426b927\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.2-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — Reveal: Four Typical Situations and Their Behaviors</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"81db9059df\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.2-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Not personality: these are four typical wall situations, and the behaviors each one produces</li>\n<li>Now unpack what O / C / U / W actually stand for:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O — Compliant</strong> (Saul)</li>\n<li><strong>U — Can't Do It</strong> (Moses)</li>\n<li><strong>W — Competitive</strong> (Jacob)</li>\n<li><strong>C — Critical</strong> (Judas)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Each tied to a biblical figure who displayed that behavior pattern</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b42847aa2a\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.2-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — The Four Situations at a Glance</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"fd0f577e0c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.2-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>For each type, briefly: core message (\"I'm not enough,\" \"I have to perform,\" etc.) and manipulator-type (passive, indifferent, competitive, active)</li>\n<li>Strengths and weaknesses come in depth with their wall: Competitive and Critical on Wednesday, Compliant and Can't Do It on Thursday</li>\n<li>Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1e31708868\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.2-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — Not Mutually Exclusive — and Not Who You Are</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"76d779bfc2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.2-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Thompson's own conclusion: most people carry elements of more than one type, and can shift over time (he describes his own shift from Compliant toward Competitive)</li>\n<li>Reassure the room: having a \"high score\" in more than one type is completely normal</li>\n<li>State it plainly: a high score names a <strong>wall you built to protect yourself</strong>, not your identity — it's easy, over time, to mistake the brick pattern for the self underneath it, but it isn't the self</li>\n<li>Quick show of hands (optional, low-pressure): who scored highest in more than one type? Normalize it visibly</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"528df34631\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.2 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.2-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — Recap: Day 2 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"81031b6a88\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.2 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.2-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.2 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.2 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap: Love Deficit → the generational pattern (visiting vs. blessing) → the two walls at a glance → your own questionnaire score → the four typical situations, their behaviors and their biblical archetypes</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind one wall in depth — Rebellion — and the restoration begins, with your first one-on-one time\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.3", "title": "Day 3 — The Wall of Rebellion: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) / Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones", "order": 4, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day3.md", "kind": "Day 3"}, "file": "d3-day3.md", "notes": ["<p>original title (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"Day 3 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 2, then explores what happens \"behind\" the Wall of Rejection when it goes unaddressed — the Lust Trap and the Flight Swing. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers the mirror-image pattern behind the Wall of Rebellion, plus the convergence point where both swings can lead toward the same destructive end.\"</p>\n", "<p>moved (Restructure, 2026-11-18): the Lust Trap / Flight Swing (old Day 3 Part 1) and the Final Swing (old Slide 16) moved to Day 4 Part 1. Part 2 here is the old Day 4 Part 1 (restoration, Rods 1–2) plus the one-on-one setup.</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## PART 1 — The Lust Trap (Flight Swing)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 2 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 5 of 9) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls' bricks → the questionnaire → the four situations and their behaviors (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls' bricks → the questionnaire → the four structures and their behaviours (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Today's turn: \"what happens when these walls stay unaddressed long enough\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 2): \"### Slide 13 — The Wall of Rebellion\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 2): \"### Slide 14 — Wall of Rebellion — Brick Inventory\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 2 — Behind the Wall\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Today: Flight. This afternoon we pick up with Fight\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 11 — The Pride Trap\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 12 — [story about xxx — a time you (or someone close to you) tried to out-perform or out-control a feeling of inadequacy, and what it actually cost]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 13 — The Fight Swing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Same oscillation structure as this morning's Flight Swing, but expressed as control/domination on one side and collapse/burnout on the other\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 14 — Secular Humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 15 — [exercise] Spot the Superman Message — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 9 — What's Coming in Part 2\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the mirror-image pattern behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## PART 2 — The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) and the Final Swing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 15, the 5th of 9 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with the full Day 3 recap and time for questions.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 10 — Opening: What's Next\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Quick, light mention only: \"we looked at Flight this morning — now let's look at its mirror image, Fight\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 17 — Why Awareness Isn't Enough\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 2 — A Change in Direction\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"- Name the shift explicitly: the first three days named the problem; today begins the solution\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 4 — Refined as Gold, Shaped as Clay\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 5 — [story about xxx — a moment in your own life where genuine change only began after a shift in your relationship with God, rather than through sheer willpower]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 6 — [exercise] Naming One Brick — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"- Not shared aloud — for the individual's own use in the prayer ministry time later\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 7 — The Seven Reinforcing Rods — Overview\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"- Today: the first two. Tomorrow: the rest\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 8 — Rod 1: Revelation\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 9 — Rod 2: Repentance\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 10 — Two Kinds of Repentance, One Destination\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 18 — Setting Up Personal Ministry Time\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"- Practical instructions for the afternoon's Personal Ministry Time (PMT): quiet space, prayer partners or ministry team availability, confidentiality expectations\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"- Encourage using today's private journaling (the named brick, the repentance sentence, the forgiveness list) as the starting material for that time\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 18 — Recap: Day 3 in Review + Questions\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Full recap: behind the Wall of Rejection — the Lust Trap and Flight Swing; behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing; both capable of converging toward the same destructive end if left unaddressed\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow, we stop diagnosing and start rebuilding\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"- Close in prayer, transitioning into Personal Ministry Time\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.3 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "be05b54576", "text": "Day 3 — The Wall of Rebellion: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) / Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "D.3 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "59ca3ee503", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 2, then goes in depth behind one wall: the Wall of Rebellion, its cornerstone pride, the Pride Trap and Fight Swing, the \"Superman Philosophy\", the Competitive and Critical situations, and the remedy, humility. Afternoon session (Part 2) begins the restoration — the shift from diagnosis to rebuilding, the first two reinforcing rods (Revelation and Repentance) — and leads into the first one-on-one times: each student with one guide, working with the Holy Spirit through their own issues."}, {"ref": "D.3 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "7d572771e5", "text": "PART 1 — The Wall of Rebellion and the Pride Trap (Fight Swing)"}, {"ref": "D.3 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "e71805417a", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 2 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 8 of 11) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.3 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.3 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "67a88bf22d", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls at a glance → your questionnaire score → the four situations and their behaviors (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical) Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"today we go behind one wall in depth — Rebellion — and this afternoon the restoration begins\""}, {"ref": "D.3 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "101612d7ee", "text": "Slide 2 — The Wall of Rebellion — In Depth"}, {"ref": "D.3 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dea3aab414", "text": "Cornerstone: pride Terminus: hostility, ultimately homicide — again, said carefully and pastorally Same three-category structure: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit Present the fuller brick list by category Note the recognizable ones: Competitive, Controlling, Resentment, Criticism"}, {"ref": "D.3 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "6c636a3c45", "text": "Slide 3 — Behind the Wall"}, {"ref": "D.3 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "0edd2826ab", "text": "Introduce the idea that walls aren't static — unaddressed pain pushes people toward coping mechanisms Two main patterns: the Flight response (behind Rejection) and the Fight response (behind Rebellion) Today: Fight. Tomorrow: Flight"}, {"ref": "D.3 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "afc0dba1cd", "text": "Slide 4 — The Pride Trap"}, {"ref": "D.3 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "72d844f68a", "text": "Define pride here as self-sufficiency — \"I don't need to be loved, I'll make myself matter\" Connects back to the Wall of Rebellion's pride cornerstone The trap: the more control/achievement is grasped for, the less secure the person actually feels underneath"}, {"ref": "D.3 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e7a1c07ed4", "text": "Slide 5 — [story about xxx — a time you (or someone close to you) tried to out-perform or out-control a feeling of inadequacy, and what it actually cost]"}, {"ref": "D.3 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "17656df782", "text": "Slide 6 — The Fight Swing"}, {"ref": "D.3 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "aa91ab3af7", "text": "An oscillation between two extremes rather than a stable middle, expressed as control/domination on one side and collapse/burnout on the other (tomorrow's Flight Swing is its mirror image) Illustrate with a simple, recognizable example (workaholism swinging into burnout, or control swinging into an outburst)"}, {"ref": "D.3 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "23fa0d878e", "text": "Slide 7 — Secular Humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\""}, {"ref": "D.3 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c8d7a402c9", "text": "Present the book's critique of the cultural \"you can be anything, master your own destiny\" narrative Point out how this philosophy can quietly reinforce the Rebellion wall by treating self-sufficiency as the goal rather than the problem Contrast with the biblical picture of strength found in humility and dependence on God"}, {"ref": "D.3 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "27a61b5b5f", "text": "Slide 8 — [exercise] Spot the Superman Message — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.3 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f5d93a3fb8", "text": "Quick group activity: look at a few real advertising slogans or popular quotes (\"Be your own hero,\" \"You don't need anyone\") and discuss, as a group, how each one either affirms healthy confidence or quietly feeds self-sufficiency Keep it light and observational, not preachy — a good energetic reset at the halfway point"}, {"ref": "D.3 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "28db7a677e", "text": "Slide 9 — Competitive and Critical in Depth"}, {"ref": "D.3 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "34281f189a", "text": "The two situations that grow from the Wall of Rebellion — for each: core message, key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type W — Competitive (Jacob, \"the supplanter\"): performs for love and acceptance. Core message: \"I'm perfect. You have no option but to love me.\" Strengths: enthusiastic, productive, an achiever. Weaknesses: can't say no, constant activity, burns out into depression between performance spurts. Competitive manipulator C — Critical (Judas): has \"lost faith in love\" and attacks others to prove they, too, are unlovable. Core message: \"I'm unlovable, and so are you.\" Strengths (the redeemed expression): loyal, committed, principled. Weaknesses: judgmental, defensive, legalistic. Active manipulator Both stand on the same cornerstone: pride Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in"}, {"ref": "D.3 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "f690c1f15b", "text": "Slide 10 — The Remedy: Humility"}, {"ref": "D.3 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-11-1", "kind": "list", "h": "a1c6e389cc", "text": "The book's remedy for pride is humility — Greek tapeinos , \"low-lying in spirit\" Not self-contempt, but dependence: the strength found in humility and dependence on God that Slide 7 contrasted with the Superman message Pride says \"I'll make myself matter\"; humility receives being loved Sets up this afternoon: Repentance (Rod 2) is where we turn from pride"}, {"ref": "D.3 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "378b53e0d2", "text": "Slide 11 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.3 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-12-1", "kind": "list", "h": "881745a379", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the restoration begins — the first two rods, Revelation and Repentance — and then the first one-on-one times Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.3 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.3 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2b747a407b", "text": "PART 2 — Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "D.3 §14 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-14-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "3bccd15506", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 17, the 6th of 12 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 3 recap and time for questions, leading into the one-on-one times."}, {"ref": "D.3 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "34a2ce90cc", "text": "Slide 12 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.3 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-15-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c31df717e3", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"we looked behind the Wall of Rebellion this morning — before we look behind the other wall tomorrow, the restoration begins\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on"}, {"ref": "D.3 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "8867de1129", "text": "Slide 13 — Why Awareness Isn't Enough"}, {"ref": "D.3 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c3595f7022", "text": "Bridge point: understanding the walls and the swings intellectually doesn't dismantle them What's needed is restoration, not just recognition — sets up the whole second half of the week Quiet moment: without sharing, let each person silently name (in their own mind or journal) one pattern from today they'd like to see change"}, {"ref": "D.3 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "90822b1bdf", "text": "Slide 14 — A Change in Direction"}, {"ref": "D.3 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "3bd4dda271", "text": "Name the shift explicitly: Monday, Tuesday and this morning named the problem; this afternoon begins the solution Encourage the room: nothing named this week is beyond repair — that's the whole point of a \"Wall of Salvation\""}, {"ref": "D.3 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "579c55796c", "text": "Slide 15 — Refined as Gold, Shaped as Clay"}, {"ref": "D.3 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "8da0a23146", "text": "Two biblical images for how God works on a person — not the same as demolishing and starting over: Gold refined by fire (Malachi 3:3, 1 Peter 1:7): heat removes dross, but the gold itself isn't destroyed — it's purified Clay shaped by the Potter (Jeremiah 18, Isaiah 64:8): still being formed, still in the Potter's hands — not abandoned to shape itself Apply directly to the wall: protection was good and necessary when the bricks were laid — but some bricks need to go, some need adjusting, some need replacing. That's refining and reshaping, not rejection The key reassurance under both images: God doesn't leave us to ourselves. He stays engaged with the material the whole time"}, {"ref": "D.3 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "11f4e462fc", "text": "Slide 16 — [story about xxx — a moment in your own life where genuine change only began after a shift in your relationship with God, rather than through sheer willpower]"}, {"ref": "D.3 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ad584c7058", "text": "Slide 17 — [exercise] Naming One Brick — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.3 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "7eb84e3d80", "text": "Quiet, personal exercise paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: using this week's teaching and questionnaire score, have each person privately write down one specific brick they now see clearly in their own wall Frame it as gold to be refined or clay to be reshaped — not a flaw in who they are Not shared aloud — for the individual's own use in this afternoon's one-on-one time"}, {"ref": "D.3 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a501be7019", "text": "Slide 18 — The Seven Reinforcing Rods — Overview"}, {"ref": "D.3 §21 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-21-1", "kind": "list", "h": "5a3b5c79cb", "text": "Preview all seven rods briefly so the room can see the whole road ahead: Revelation, Repentance, Release, Recognize, Renounce, Renewing, Rebuilding Today: Revelation and Repentance. Tomorrow: Release, Recognize, Renounce. Friday: Renewing and Rebuilding"}, {"ref": "D.3 §22 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-22-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "7fd34c3aa0", "text": "Slide 19 — Rod 1: Revelation"}, {"ref": "D.3 §22 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-22-1", "kind": "list", "h": "3aae72b618", "text": "Define: seeing your wall and its bricks clearly, in God's light rather than in self-condemnation or denial This week's teaching and the questionnaire have already been a form of revelation — today gives it a name and a next step Key distinction: revelation exposes to heal, not to shame — and exposes a brick, not a self"}, {"ref": "D.3 §23 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-23-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "de1c820559", "text": "Slide 20 — Rod 2: Repentance"}, {"ref": "D.3 §23 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-23-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6ae9cf496c", "text": "Define repentance as a change of mind that leads to a change of direction — not just feeling sorry Rejection's cornerstone: unbelief — repentance here means turning from unbelief about one's identity in God Rebellion's cornerstone: pride — repentance here means turning from self-sufficiency toward dependence on God Today the weight is on pride (this morning's remedy, humility); tomorrow we bring the same rod to unbelief and God-declared worth"}, {"ref": "D.3 §24 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-24-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "821905c3dc", "text": "Slide 21 — Two Kinds of Repentance, One Destination"}, {"ref": "D.3 §24 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-24-1", "kind": "list", "h": "407f95cc05", "text": "Contrast how repentance looks different depending on which wall someone leans toward (Rejection vs. Rebellion) but arrives at the same place: trusting God's plumbline over the false ones named on Day 1 Reinforces the whole week's throughline back to the Amos 7 vision Close with a guided journaling prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): using the brick named earlier, write a short, honest sentence of repentance — not generic, but specific to what was revealed; optional to share with a trusted partner"}, {"ref": "D.3 §25 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-25-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ef9e56f294", "text": "Slide 22 — Setting Up the One-on-One Times"}, {"ref": "D.3 §25 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-25-1", "kind": "list", "h": "76615ff4af", "text": "Practical instructions for the afternoon's one-on-one times: each student meets with one guide; a quiet space; confidentiality expectations The guide doesn't fix anyone: student and guide work with the Holy Spirit through the student's own issues Listening prayer: ask Him to show one brick, and the lie behind it Respond with the relevant rods — today Revelation (seeing it in God's light) and Repentance (turning from it) Encourage using today's private journaling (the named brick, the repentance sentence) as the starting material for that time"}, {"ref": "D.3 §26 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.3-26-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "03c6458449", "text": "Slide 23 — Recap: Day 3 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.3 §26 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.3-26-1", "kind": "list", "h": "454ded6911", "text": "Full recap: behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing, the Superman message, Competitive and Critical, the remedy of humility; then the shift from diagnosis to restoration, gold and clay, Rod 1 Revelation and Rod 2 Repentance Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind the other wall — Rejection — and continue the rods: Release, Recognize, Renounce\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer, transitioning into the one-on-one times"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"be05b54576\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.3-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 3 — The Wall of Rebellion: The Pride Trap (Fight Swing) / Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"59ca3ee503\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.3-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 2, then goes in depth behind one wall: the Wall of Rebellion, its cornerstone pride, the Pride Trap and Fight Swing, the \"Superman Philosophy\", the Competitive and Critical situations, and the remedy, humility. Afternoon session (Part 2) begins the restoration — the shift from diagnosis to rebuilding, the first two reinforcing rods (Revelation and Repentance) — and leads into the first one-on-one times: each student with one guide, working with the Holy Spirit through their own issues.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7d572771e5\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.3-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — The Wall of Rebellion and the Pride Trap (Fight Swing)</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e71805417a\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.3-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 2 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 8 of 11) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.3-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"67a88bf22d\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.3-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 2: Love Deficit and the generational pattern → the two walls at a glance → your questionnaire score → the four situations and their behaviors (Compliant, Can't Do It, Competitive, Critical)</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"today we go behind one wall in depth — Rebellion — and this afternoon the restoration begins\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"101612d7ee\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.3-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — The Wall of Rebellion — In Depth</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dea3aab414\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.3-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Cornerstone: pride</li>\n<li>Terminus: hostility, ultimately homicide — again, said carefully and pastorally</li>\n<li>Same three-category structure: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit</li>\n<li>Present the fuller brick list by category</li>\n<li>Note the recognizable ones: Competitive, Controlling, Resentment, Criticism</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6c636a3c45\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.3-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — Behind the Wall</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0edd2826ab\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.3-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce the idea that walls aren't static — unaddressed pain pushes people toward coping mechanisms</li>\n<li>Two main patterns: the Flight response (behind Rejection) and the Fight response (behind Rebellion)</li>\n<li>Today: Fight. Tomorrow: Flight</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"afc0dba1cd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.3-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — The Pride Trap</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"72d844f68a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.3-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define pride here as self-sufficiency — \"I don't need to be loved, I'll make myself matter\"</li>\n<li>Connects back to the Wall of Rebellion's pride cornerstone</li>\n<li>The trap: the more control/achievement is grasped for, the less secure the person actually feels underneath</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e7a1c07ed4\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.3-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a time you (or someone close to you) tried to out-perform or out-control a feeling of inadequacy, and what it actually cost]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"17656df782\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.3-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — The Fight Swing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"aa91ab3af7\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.3-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>An oscillation between two extremes rather than a stable middle, expressed as control/domination on one side and collapse/burnout on the other (tomorrow's Flight Swing is its mirror image)</li>\n<li>Illustrate with a simple, recognizable example (workaholism swinging into burnout, or control swinging into an outburst)</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"23fa0d878e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.3-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — Secular Humanism's \"Superman Philosophy\"</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c8d7a402c9\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.3-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Present the book's critique of the cultural \"you can be anything, master your own destiny\" narrative</li>\n<li>Point out how this philosophy can quietly reinforce the Rebellion wall by treating self-sufficiency as the goal rather than the problem</li>\n<li>Contrast with the biblical picture of strength found in humility and dependence on God</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"27a61b5b5f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.3-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Spot the Superman Message — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f5d93a3fb8\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.3-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick group activity: look at a few real advertising slogans or popular quotes (\"Be your own hero,\" \"You don't need anyone\") and discuss, as a group, how each one either affirms healthy confidence or quietly feeds self-sufficiency</li>\n<li>Keep it light and observational, not preachy — a good energetic reset at the halfway point</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"28db7a677e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.3-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — Competitive and Critical in Depth</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"34281f189a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.3-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The two situations that grow from the Wall of Rebellion — for each: core message, key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type\n<ul>\n<li><strong>W — Competitive</strong> (Jacob, \"the supplanter\"): performs for love and acceptance. Core message: \"I'm perfect. You have no option but to love me.\" Strengths: enthusiastic, productive, an achiever. Weaknesses: can't say no, constant activity, burns out into depression between performance spurts. Competitive manipulator</li>\n<li><strong>C — Critical</strong> (Judas): has \"lost faith in love\" and attacks others to prove they, too, are unlovable. Core message: \"I'm unlovable, and so are you.\" Strengths (the redeemed expression): loyal, committed, principled. Weaknesses: judgmental, defensive, legalistic. Active manipulator</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Both stand on the same cornerstone: pride</li>\n<li>Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f690c1f15b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.3-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — The Remedy: Humility</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a1c6e389cc\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.3-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The book's remedy for pride is humility — Greek <em>tapeinos</em>, \"low-lying in spirit\"</li>\n<li>Not self-contempt, but dependence: the strength found in humility and dependence on God that Slide 7 contrasted with the Superman message</li>\n<li>Pride says \"I'll make myself matter\"; humility receives being loved</li>\n<li>Sets up this afternoon: Repentance (Rod 2) is where we turn from pride</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"378b53e0d2\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.3-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"881745a379\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.3-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the restoration begins — the first two rods, Revelation and Repentance — and then the first one-on-one times</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.3-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2b747a407b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.3-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Restoration Begins: Revelation, Repentance, and One-on-Ones</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3bccd15506\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §14 ¶1\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.3-14-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §14 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §14 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 17, the 6th of 12 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 3 recap and time for questions, leading into the one-on-one times.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"34a2ce90cc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.3-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c31df717e3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.3-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"we looked behind the Wall of Rebellion this morning — before we look behind the other wall tomorrow, the restoration begins\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8867de1129\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.3-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — Why Awareness Isn't Enough</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c3595f7022\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.3-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Bridge point: understanding the walls and the swings intellectually doesn't dismantle them</li>\n<li>What's needed is restoration, not just recognition — sets up the whole second half of the week</li>\n<li>Quiet moment: without sharing, let each person silently name (in their own mind or journal) one pattern from today they'd like to see change</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"90822b1bdf\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.3-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — A Change in Direction</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3bd4dda271\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.3-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Name the shift explicitly: Monday, Tuesday and this morning named the problem; this afternoon begins the solution</li>\n<li>Encourage the room: nothing named this week is beyond repair — that's the whole point of a \"Wall of Salvation\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"579c55796c\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.3-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — Refined as Gold, Shaped as Clay</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8da0a23146\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.3-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Two biblical images for how God works on a person — not the same as demolishing and starting over:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gold refined by fire</strong> (Malachi 3:3, 1 Peter 1:7): heat removes dross, but the gold itself isn't destroyed — it's purified</li>\n<li><strong>Clay shaped by the Potter</strong> (Jeremiah 18, Isaiah 64:8): still being formed, still in the Potter's hands — not abandoned to shape itself</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Apply directly to the wall: protection was good and necessary when the bricks were laid — but some bricks need to go, some need adjusting, some need replacing. That's refining and reshaping, not rejection</li>\n<li>The key reassurance under both images: <strong>God doesn't leave us to ourselves.</strong> He stays engaged with the material the whole time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"11f4e462fc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.3-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a moment in your own life where genuine change only began after a shift in your relationship with God, rather than through sheer willpower]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ad584c7058\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.3-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Naming One Brick — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7eb84e3d80\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.3-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quiet, personal exercise paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: using this week's teaching and questionnaire score, have each person privately write down one specific brick they now see clearly in their own wall</li>\n<li>Frame it as gold to be refined or clay to be reshaped — not a flaw in who they are</li>\n<li>Not shared aloud — for the individual's own use in this afternoon's one-on-one time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a501be7019\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.3-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — The Seven Reinforcing Rods — Overview</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5a3b5c79cb\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §21 ¶1\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.3-21-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §21 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §21 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Preview all seven rods briefly so the room can see the whole road ahead: Revelation, Repentance, Release, Recognize, Renounce, Renewing, Rebuilding</li>\n<li>Today: Revelation and Repentance. Tomorrow: Release, Recognize, Renounce. Friday: Renewing and Rebuilding</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7fd34c3aa0\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §22 ¶0\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.3-22-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §22 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §22 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 19 — Rod 1: Revelation</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3aae72b618\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §22 ¶1\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.3-22-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §22 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §22 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define: seeing your wall and its bricks clearly, in God's light rather than in self-condemnation or denial</li>\n<li>This week's teaching and the questionnaire have already been a form of revelation — today gives it a name and a next step</li>\n<li>Key distinction: revelation exposes to heal, not to shame — and exposes a brick, not a self</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"de1c820559\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §23 ¶0\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.3-23-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §23 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §23 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 20 — Rod 2: Repentance</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6ae9cf496c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §23 ¶1\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.3-23-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §23 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §23 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define repentance as a change of mind that leads to a change of direction — not just feeling sorry</li>\n<li>Rejection's cornerstone: unbelief — repentance here means turning from unbelief about one's identity in God</li>\n<li>Rebellion's cornerstone: pride — repentance here means turning from self-sufficiency toward dependence on God</li>\n<li>Today the weight is on pride (this morning's remedy, humility); tomorrow we bring the same rod to unbelief and God-declared worth</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"821905c3dc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §24 ¶0\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.3-24-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §24 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §24 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 21 — Two Kinds of Repentance, One Destination</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"407f95cc05\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §24 ¶1\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.3-24-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §24 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §24 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Contrast how repentance looks different depending on which wall someone leans toward (Rejection vs. Rebellion) but arrives at the same place: trusting God's plumbline over the false ones named on Day 1</li>\n<li>Reinforces the whole week's throughline back to the Amos 7 vision</li>\n<li>Close with a guided journaling prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): using the brick named earlier, write a short, honest sentence of repentance — not generic, but specific to what was revealed; optional to share with a trusted partner</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ef9e56f294\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §25 ¶0\" data-sec=\"25\" id=\"p-D.3-25-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §25 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §25 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§25 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 22 — Setting Up the One-on-One Times</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"76615ff4af\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §25 ¶1\" data-sec=\"25\" id=\"p-D.3-25-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §25 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §25 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§25 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Practical instructions for the afternoon's one-on-one times: each student meets with one guide; a quiet space; confidentiality expectations</li>\n<li>The guide doesn't fix anyone: student and guide work with the Holy Spirit through the student's own issues\n<ul>\n<li>Listening prayer: ask Him to show one brick, and the lie behind it</li>\n<li>Respond with the relevant rods — today Revelation (seeing it in God's light) and Repentance (turning from it)</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Encourage using today's private journaling (the named brick, the repentance sentence) as the starting material for that time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"03c6458449\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.3 §26 ¶0\" data-sec=\"26\" id=\"p-D.3-26-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §26 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §26 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§26 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 23 — Recap: Day 3 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"454ded6911\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.3 §26 ¶1\" data-sec=\"26\" id=\"p-D.3-26-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.3 §26 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.3 §26 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§26 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap: behind the Wall of Rebellion — the Pride Trap and Fight Swing, the Superman message, Competitive and Critical, the remedy of humility; then the shift from diagnosis to restoration, gold and clay, Rod 1 Revelation and Rod 2 Repentance</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we go behind the other wall — Rejection — and continue the rods: Release, Recognize, Renounce\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer, transitioning into the one-on-one times</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.4", "title": "Day 4 — The Wall of Rejection: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones", "order": 5, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day4.md", "kind": "Day 4"}, "file": "d4-day4.md", "notes": ["<p>original title (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"Day 4 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance / Release, Recognize, and Renounce\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 3, then shifts the whole tone of the week from diagnosis to restoration — the Wall of Salvation, and the first two \"reinforcing rods\": Revelation and Repentance. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 3 (Release/Forgiveness) and Rods 4–5 (Recognize and Renounce Satan's tactics), leading into personal prayer ministry time.\"</p>\n", "<p>moved (Restructure, 2026-11-18): old Day 4 Part 1 (A Change in Direction, Gold and Clay, its story, Naming One Brick, the rods overview, Rods 1–2) moved to Day 3 Part 2; the Altar and the Temple (old Slide 3) moved to Day 5 Slide 3. Part 1 here is the old Day 3 Part 1 (Lust Trap, Flight Swing) plus the Wall of Rejection (old Day 2 Slides 11–12) and the Final Swing (old Day 3 Slide 16).</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## PART 1 — Restoration Begins: Revelation and Repentance\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 3 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 6 of 11) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- One-slide reminder of Day 3: the Lust Trap/Flight Swing behind Rejection; the Pride Trap/Fight Swing behind Rebellion; both able to converge toward the same destructive end if unaddressed\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Today's turn: \"today the whole tone changes — from diagnosis to rebuilding\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 2): \"### Slide 11 — The Wall of Rejection\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 2): \"### Slide 12 — Wall of Rejection — Brick Inventory\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 3 — The Lust Trap\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 4 — [story about xxx — a personal or observed example of reaching for something (not necessarily addiction — could be food, media, approval-seeking, etc.) to numb pain rather than face it]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 5 — [exercise] Naming the Swing — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 6 — The Flight Swing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3 Slide 10): \"- Reminder: same underlying wound, opposite direction of response\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 7 — Eating Disorders as a Case Study\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 8 — Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"### Slide 16 — The Final Swing: Where Both Walls Can Lead\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 3): \"- Purpose of naming this: not to alarm, but to show why the restoration process (starting tomorrow) matters\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 11 — What's Coming in Part 2\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, forgiveness (Release) and confronting the enemy's tactics (Recognize/Renounce)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## PART 2 — Release, Recognize, and Renounce\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 15, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 4 recap and time for questions, leading into Personal Ministry Time.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 12 — Opening: What's Next\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 13 — Rod 3: Release (Forgiveness)\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Private journaling prompt (not shared): write down one or two names of people (including possibly yourself) that came to mind during this week's teaching — preparation for today's Personal Ministry Time, between the individual and God, not a public confession\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 14 — [story about xxx — a real forgiveness journey of your own, including how long it took and what changed once you finally released it]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 15 — [exercise] Rods 4–5: Recognize and Renounce — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 16 — What Renouncing Looks Like\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 17 — Tying the Rods Together\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 18 — Setting Up Personal Ministry Time\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Practical instructions for the afternoon's Personal Ministry Time (PMT): quiet space, prayer partners or ministry team availability, confidentiality expectations\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Encourage using today's private journaling (the named brick, the repentance sentence, the forgiveness list) as the starting material for that time\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 19 — Recap: Day 4 in Review + Questions\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Full recap: the shift from diagnosis to restoration; the Altar and Temple; Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance, Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we finish the wall — Renewing and Rebuilding, and we close out the week together\"\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Close in prayer, transitioning into Personal Ministry Time\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.4 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "68558001b6", "text": "Day 4 — The Wall of Rejection: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "D.4 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "f00f760077", "text": "Day overview: Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 3, then goes in depth behind the other wall: the Wall of Rejection, its cornerstone unbelief, the Lust Trap and Flight Swing (with eating disorders as a case study and Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory), the Compliant and Can't Do It situations, the remedy, God-declared worth — and the Final Swing, where both swings can converge. Afternoon session (Part 2) continues the rods with Rod 3 (Release/Forgiveness) and Rods 4–5 (Recognize and Renounce Satan's tactics), leading into the second round of one-on-one times."}, {"ref": "D.4 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "82de0148ae", "text": "PART 1 — The Wall of Rejection, the Lust Trap (Flight Swing), and the Final Swing"}, {"ref": "D.4 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1fd7f7745a", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 3 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day, including from yesterday's one-on-one times. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 6 of 12) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.4 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.4 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "33e8de9b76", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 3: the Wall of Rebellion, the Pride Trap/Fight Swing and the remedy of humility; then restoration begun — Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance — and yesterday's one-on-one times Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"today we go behind the other wall — Rejection — and this afternoon we continue the rods\""}, {"ref": "D.4 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c8d5847b87", "text": "Slide 2 — The Wall of Rejection — In Depth"}, {"ref": "D.4 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "910ace866d", "text": "Cornerstone: unbelief Terminus (end state if unaddressed): despair, ultimately suicide — said gently and pastorally, not alarmist Introduce the three brick categories: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit Present the fuller brick list by category (Emotions / Intellect / Spirit) from the book Don't over-explain each one yet — this is a naming slide, not yet a diagnosis slide Point out a few that people will recognize immediately (e.g., Depression, Apathy, Inferiority)"}, {"ref": "D.4 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "3e336af7e3", "text": "Slide 3 — Compliant and Can't Do It in Depth"}, {"ref": "D.4 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "bd503e5517", "text": "The two situations that grow from the Wall of Rejection — for each: core message, key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type O — Compliant (Saul): reaches toward authority figures. Core message: \"I'll do anything you want! Please love me.\" Strengths: a server, sensitive, caring. Weaknesses: man-pleasing, procrastination, collapses into rejection when an authority figure lets them down. Passive manipulator U — Can't Do It (Moses at the burning bush: \"I can't!\"): withdraws from authority figures after too many setbacks. Core message: \"Nobody loves me. I give up.\" Strengths: intuitive, compassionate, creative. Weaknesses: moody, self-pity, shame, escapism. Indifferent manipulator Both stand on the same cornerstone: unbelief Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in"}, {"ref": "D.4 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "04290e9514", "text": "Slide 4 — The Lust Trap"}, {"ref": "D.4 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "1150508f06", "text": "Define \"lust\" broadly here — not only sexual, but any craving used to numb or escape pain The trap: what promises relief actually deepens the wound it's trying to escape Connects directly back to the Wall of Rejection's unbelief cornerstone — \"I am not enough, so I'll fill the gap myself\""}, {"ref": "D.4 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d1fc310141", "text": "Slide 5 — [story about xxx — a personal or observed example of reaching for something (not necessarily addiction — could be food, media, approval-seeking, etc.) to numb pain rather than face it]"}, {"ref": "D.4 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e61ab85875", "text": "Slide 6 — [exercise] Naming the Swing — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.4 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dbb9acab32", "text": "Quiet, personal reflection paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: think of an area where you notice yourself swinging between two extremes (not necessarily food) — indulge vs. deny, engage vs. avoid Not shared aloud — purely for personal insight, but gives a physical break at the halfway mark"}, {"ref": "D.4 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "333926e69d", "text": "Slide 7 — The Flight Swing"}, {"ref": "D.4 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "008d990392", "text": "Explain the \"swing\" metaphor: an oscillation between two extremes rather than a stable middle Flight swing: between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal Neither extreme resolves the underlying wound — both are reactions to it The mirror image of yesterday's Fight Swing: same underlying wound, opposite direction of response"}, {"ref": "D.4 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "ccafbb45cc", "text": "Slide 8 — Eating Disorders as a Case Study"}, {"ref": "D.4 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "0207b92ee6", "text": "Use the book's discussion of eating disorders as a concrete illustration of the Flight Swing in action Control/restriction on one side, bingeing/numbing on the other — same underlying wound, different expressions"}, {"ref": "D.4 §9 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.4-9-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1db302afbe", "text": "Delivery note: likely someone in the room has a personal connection to this topic — a pastoral, unhurried tone here matters more than the content itself."}, {"ref": "D.4 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "90bf8bf006", "text": "Slide 9 — Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory"}, {"ref": "D.4 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dee4932e2c", "text": "Introduce Solomon's psychological theory referenced in the book: every strong pleasure is followed by an opposite \"counter-emotion,\" and repeated exposure trains the swing to get more extreme Simple example: the crash after a high, and why the craving intensifies over time Bridges the psychological research to the spiritual pattern already described Close with a quick verbal prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): in general terms, what has helped people you know break an unhealthy pattern?"}, {"ref": "D.4 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d79cadf698", "text": "Slide 10 — The Remedy: God-Declared Worth"}, {"ref": "D.4 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-11-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d3183b7e06", "text": "The book's remedy for unbelief: grasping your God-declared worth — yesterday's Rod 2, Repentance, now brought to the Wall of Rejection The book's illustration: what a human body is worth in raw chemicals, set against the price God paid for you — the blood of Christ Worth isn't earned (the Compliant) or given up on (Can't Do It) — it is declared by God"}, {"ref": "D.4 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b5813ca474", "text": "Slide 11 — The Final Swing: Where Both Walls Can Lead"}, {"ref": "D.4 §12 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-12-1", "kind": "list", "h": "2fb41b7120", "text": "Explain the book's \"soul swings\" concept: both the Flight and Fight patterns, left unaddressed long enough, can converge toward the same destructive endpoint — described in the book as a kind of double-mindedness Purpose of naming this: not to alarm, but to show why the restoration process (begun yesterday, continued this afternoon) matters Reiterate: naming the pattern is not a diagnosis of anyone in the room — it's a warning label on an unaddressed road, not a prediction"}, {"ref": "D.4 §12 ¶2", "anchor": "p-D.4-12-2", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "33da7cd010", "text": "Delivery note: a careful, pastoral tone matters more here than on most other slides this week."}, {"ref": "D.4 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "472eebbf19", "text": "Slide 12 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.4 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4c6b8871cd", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, forgiveness (Release) and confronting the enemy's tactics (Recognize/Renounce), then the one-on-one times Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.4 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.4 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c914246fb1", "text": "PART 2 — Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones"}, {"ref": "D.4 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-15-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "ee87b83f56", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 16, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 4 recap and time for questions, leading into the one-on-one times."}, {"ref": "D.4 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "51f6353cff", "text": "Slide 13 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.4 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9f42c646d6", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"revelation showed us the brick, repentance turned our heart — now we deal with what's owed to others, and what's coming against us\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on"}, {"ref": "D.4 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "eff682670d", "text": "Slide 14 — Rod 3: Release (Forgiveness)"}, {"ref": "D.4 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "dbc7d19898", "text": "Define forgiveness biblically: releasing a debt, not excusing an offense or pretending it didn't happen Link back to Day 2's generational pattern teaching: unforgiveness is often exactly how generational wounds get passed on Key reassurance: forgiveness is a decision, not necessarily a felt emotion at first The book's study guide calls this session \"The Daily Cross\": forgiveness received and passed on, day by day — tomorrow we come back to the daily cross Private journaling prompt (not shared): write down one or two names of people (including possibly yourself) that came to mind during this week's teaching — preparation for this afternoon's one-on-one time, between the individual and God, not a public confession"}, {"ref": "D.4 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "4b24837e9e", "text": "Slide 15 — [story about xxx — a real forgiveness journey of your own, including how long it took and what changed once you finally released it]"}, {"ref": "D.4 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a76b7738dd", "text": "Slide 16 — [exercise] Rods 4–5: Recognize and Renounce — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.4 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "88df2d0950", "text": "Introduce these as a pair: first recognizing the enemy's tactics, then actively renouncing them Reference the \"four weapons\" or tactics named in the book (deception, accusation, temptation, oppression — adjust to match the actual book language) Stand-and-move moment: a short physical shift (e.g., stand up, shake it off) to mark the halfway point before this heavier topic Point: the goal isn't fear of the enemy, but clarity — an identified tactic loses most of its power"}, {"ref": "D.4 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "3181970d2e", "text": "Slide 17 — What Renouncing Looks Like"}, {"ref": "D.4 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "554d5674e0", "text": "Explain renouncing as an active, verbal, decisive break — not just an internal feeling Example structure for a renouncing statement (without being overly scripted/robotic) Reassure: this is a normal, healthy part of the restoration process, not something to fear doing \"wrong\" Practice the language first as a group, using a light, non-personal example (e.g., renouncing a bad habit like procrastination) so people are comfortable with it before applying it personally in private prayer time"}, {"ref": "D.4 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "b392862b36", "text": "Slide 18 — Tying the Rods Together"}, {"ref": "D.4 §21 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-21-1", "kind": "list", "h": "31df5ae35e", "text": "Quick visual recap of Rods 1–5 in sequence: Revelation → Repentance → Release → Recognize → Renounce Preview: tomorrow completes the picture with Renewing and Rebuilding"}, {"ref": "D.4 §22 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-22-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "592ec47d11", "text": "Slide 19 — Setting Up the One-on-One Times (Round 2)"}, {"ref": "D.4 §22 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-22-1", "kind": "list", "h": "e868f92d16", "text": "Same shape as yesterday: each student with one guide, working with the Holy Spirit through their own issues Listening prayer: ask Him to show a brick and the lie behind it — a new one, or yesterday's again Respond with the relevant rods — now Revelation, Repentance, Release (forgiving the names on your list), Recognize and Renounce Encourage using today's private journaling (the forgiveness list) and yesterday's (the named brick, the repentance sentence) as the starting material for that time"}, {"ref": "D.4 §23 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.4-23-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5340b78d79", "text": "Slide 20 — Recap: Day 4 in Review + Questions"}, {"ref": "D.4 §23 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.4-23-1", "kind": "list", "h": "56bcd70c81", "text": "Full recap: behind the Wall of Rejection — the Lust Trap and Flight Swing, Compliant and Can't Do It, the remedy of God-declared worth; the Final Swing; Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce Open the floor for questions from today's teaching Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we land — the daily cross, Renewing and Rebuilding, and we close out the week together\" Word of encouragement Close in prayer, transitioning into the one-on-one times"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"68558001b6\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.4-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 4 — The Wall of Rejection: The Lust Trap (Flight Swing) / Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f00f760077\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.4-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 3, then goes in depth behind the other wall: the Wall of Rejection, its cornerstone unbelief, the Lust Trap and Flight Swing (with eating disorders as a case study and Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory), the Compliant and Can't Do It situations, the remedy, God-declared worth — and the Final Swing, where both swings can converge. Afternoon session (Part 2) continues the rods with Rod 3 (Release/Forgiveness) and Rods 4–5 (Recognize and Renounce Satan's tactics), leading into the second round of one-on-one times.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"82de0148ae\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.4-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — The Wall of Rejection, the Lust Trap (Flight Swing), and the Final Swing</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1fd7f7745a\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.4-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 3 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day, including from yesterday's one-on-one times. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 6 of 12) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.4-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"33e8de9b76\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.4-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 3: the Wall of Rebellion, the Pride Trap/Fight Swing and the remedy of humility; then restoration begun — Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance — and yesterday's one-on-one times</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"today we go behind the other wall — Rejection — and this afternoon we continue the rods\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c8d5847b87\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.4-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — The Wall of Rejection — In Depth</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"910ace866d\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.4-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Cornerstone: unbelief</li>\n<li>Terminus (end state if unaddressed): despair, ultimately suicide — said gently and pastorally, not alarmist</li>\n<li>Introduce the three brick categories: Emotions, Intellect, Spirit</li>\n<li>Present the fuller brick list by category (Emotions / Intellect / Spirit) from the book</li>\n<li>Don't over-explain each one yet — this is a naming slide, not yet a diagnosis slide</li>\n<li>Point out a few that people will recognize immediately (e.g., Depression, Apathy, Inferiority)</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3e336af7e3\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.4-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — Compliant and Can't Do It in Depth</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"bd503e5517\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.4-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The two situations that grow from the Wall of Rejection — for each: core message, key strengths, key weaknesses, manipulator-type\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O — Compliant</strong> (Saul): reaches <em>toward</em> authority figures. Core message: \"I'll do anything you want! Please love me.\" Strengths: a server, sensitive, caring. Weaknesses: man-pleasing, procrastination, collapses into rejection when an authority figure lets them down. Passive manipulator</li>\n<li><strong>U — Can't Do It</strong> (Moses at the burning bush: \"I can't!\"): <em>withdraws</em> from authority figures after too many setbacks. Core message: \"Nobody loves me. I give up.\" Strengths: intuitive, compassionate, creative. Weaknesses: moody, self-pity, shame, escapism. Indifferent manipulator</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Both stand on the same cornerstone: unbelief</li>\n<li>Emphasize: these are patterns to recognize, not boxes to live in</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"04290e9514\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.4-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — The Lust Trap</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1150508f06\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.4-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define \"lust\" broadly here — not only sexual, but any craving used to numb or escape pain</li>\n<li>The trap: what promises relief actually deepens the wound it's trying to escape</li>\n<li>Connects directly back to the Wall of Rejection's unbelief cornerstone — \"I am not enough, so I'll fill the gap myself\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d1fc310141\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.4-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a personal or observed example of reaching for something (not necessarily addiction — could be food, media, approval-seeking, etc.) to numb pain rather than face it]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e61ab85875\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.4-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Naming the Swing — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dbb9acab32\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.4-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quiet, personal reflection paired with a brief stand-and-stretch: think of an area where you notice yourself swinging between two extremes (not necessarily food) — indulge vs. deny, engage vs. avoid</li>\n<li>Not shared aloud — purely for personal insight, but gives a physical break at the halfway mark</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"333926e69d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.4-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — The Flight Swing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"008d990392\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.4-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain the \"swing\" metaphor: an oscillation between two extremes rather than a stable middle</li>\n<li>Flight swing: between numbing/indulgence and self-punishment/withdrawal</li>\n<li>Neither extreme resolves the underlying wound — both are reactions to it</li>\n<li>The mirror image of yesterday's Fight Swing: same underlying wound, opposite direction of response</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ccafbb45cc\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.4-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Eating Disorders as a Case Study</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0207b92ee6\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.4-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Use the book's discussion of eating disorders as a concrete illustration of the Flight Swing in action</li>\n<li>Control/restriction on one side, bingeing/numbing on the other — same underlying wound, different expressions</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1db302afbe\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.4 §9 ¶2\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.4-9-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §9 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §9 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: likely someone in the room has a personal connection to this topic — a pastoral, unhurried tone here matters more than the content itself.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"90bf8bf006\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.4-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — Solomon's Opponent-Process Theory</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dee4932e2c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.4-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce Solomon's psychological theory referenced in the book: every strong pleasure is followed by an opposite \"counter-emotion,\" and repeated exposure trains the swing to get more extreme</li>\n<li>Simple example: the crash after a high, and why the craving intensifies over time</li>\n<li>Bridges the psychological research to the spiritual pattern already described</li>\n<li>Close with a quick verbal prompt (not a break activity, stays seated): in general terms, what has helped people you know break an unhealthy pattern?</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d79cadf698\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.4-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — The Remedy: God-Declared Worth</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d3183b7e06\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.4-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The book's remedy for unbelief: grasping your God-declared worth — yesterday's Rod 2, Repentance, now brought to the Wall of Rejection</li>\n<li>The book's illustration: what a human body is worth in raw chemicals, set against the price God paid for you — the blood of Christ</li>\n<li>Worth isn't earned (the Compliant) or given up on (Can't Do It) — it is declared by God</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b5813ca474\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.4-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — The Final Swing: Where Both Walls Can Lead</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2fb41b7120\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §12 ¶1\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.4-12-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §12 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §12 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain the book's \"soul swings\" concept: both the Flight and Fight patterns, left unaddressed long enough, can converge toward the same destructive endpoint — described in the book as a kind of double-mindedness</li>\n<li>Purpose of naming this: not to alarm, but to show why the restoration process (begun yesterday, continued this afternoon) matters</li>\n<li>Reiterate: naming the pattern is not a diagnosis of anyone in the room — it's a warning label on an unaddressed road, not a prediction</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"33da7cd010\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"2\" data-ref=\"D.4 §12 ¶2\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.4-12-2\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §12 ¶2\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §12 ¶2 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶2</button><p data-body=\"1\"><em>Delivery note: a careful, pastoral tone matters more here than on most other slides this week.</em></p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"472eebbf19\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.4-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4c6b8871cd\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.4-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, forgiveness (Release) and confronting the enemy's tactics (Recognize/Renounce), then the one-on-one times</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.4-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c914246fb1\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.4-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Release, Recognize, Renounce, and One-on-Ones</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"ee87b83f56\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.4-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 16, the 4th of 8 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the full Day 4 recap and time for questions, leading into the one-on-one times.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"51f6353cff\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.4-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9f42c646d6\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.4-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"revelation showed us the brick, repentance turned our heart — now we deal with what's owed to others, and what's coming against us\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"eff682670d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.4-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — Rod 3: Release (Forgiveness)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dbc7d19898\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.4-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define forgiveness biblically: releasing a debt, not excusing an offense or pretending it didn't happen</li>\n<li>Link back to Day 2's generational pattern teaching: unforgiveness is often exactly how generational wounds get passed on</li>\n<li>Key reassurance: forgiveness is a decision, not necessarily a felt emotion at first</li>\n<li>The book's study guide calls this session \"The Daily Cross\": forgiveness received and passed on, day by day — tomorrow we come back to the daily cross</li>\n<li>Private journaling prompt (not shared): write down one or two names of people (including possibly yourself) that came to mind during this week's teaching — preparation for this afternoon's one-on-one time, between the individual and God, not a public confession</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4b24837e9e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.4-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a real forgiveness journey of your own, including how long it took and what changed once you finally released it]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a76b7738dd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.4-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Rods 4–5: Recognize and Renounce — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"88df2d0950\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.4-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce these as a pair: first recognizing the enemy's tactics, then actively renouncing them</li>\n<li>Reference the \"four weapons\" or tactics named in the book (deception, accusation, temptation, oppression — adjust to match the actual book language)</li>\n<li>Stand-and-move moment: a short physical shift (e.g., stand up, shake it off) to mark the halfway point before this heavier topic</li>\n<li>Point: the goal isn't fear of the enemy, but clarity — an identified tactic loses most of its power</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3181970d2e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.4-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — What Renouncing Looks Like</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"554d5674e0\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.4-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Explain renouncing as an active, verbal, decisive break — not just an internal feeling</li>\n<li>Example structure for a renouncing statement (without being overly scripted/robotic)</li>\n<li>Reassure: this is a normal, healthy part of the restoration process, not something to fear doing \"wrong\"</li>\n<li>Practice the language first as a group, using a light, non-personal example (e.g., renouncing a bad habit like procrastination) so people are comfortable with it before applying it personally in private prayer time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"b392862b36\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.4-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — Tying the Rods Together</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"31df5ae35e\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §21 ¶1\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.4-21-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §21 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §21 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick visual recap of Rods 1–5 in sequence: Revelation → Repentance → Release → Recognize → Renounce</li>\n<li>Preview: tomorrow completes the picture with Renewing and Rebuilding</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"592ec47d11\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §22 ¶0\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.4-22-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §22 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §22 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 19 — Setting Up the One-on-One Times (Round 2)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e868f92d16\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §22 ¶1\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.4-22-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §22 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §22 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Same shape as yesterday: each student with one guide, working with the Holy Spirit through their own issues\n<ul>\n<li>Listening prayer: ask Him to show a brick and the lie behind it — a new one, or yesterday's again</li>\n<li>Respond with the relevant rods — now Revelation, Repentance, Release (forgiving the names on your list), Recognize and Renounce</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Encourage using today's private journaling (the forgiveness list) and yesterday's (the named brick, the repentance sentence) as the starting material for that time</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5340b78d79\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.4 §23 ¶0\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.4-23-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §23 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §23 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 20 — Recap: Day 4 in Review + Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"56bcd70c81\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.4 §23 ¶1\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.4-23-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.4 §23 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.4 §23 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap: behind the Wall of Rejection — the Lust Trap and Flight Swing, Compliant and Can't Do It, the remedy of God-declared worth; the Final Swing; Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce</li>\n<li>Open the floor for questions from today's teaching</li>\n<li>Bridge to tomorrow: \"tomorrow we land — the daily cross, Renewing and Rebuilding, and we close out the week together\"</li>\n<li>Word of encouragement</li>\n<li>Close in prayer, transitioning into the one-on-one times</li>\n</ul></div>"}, {"id": "D.5", "title": "Day 5 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning", "order": 6, "metadata": {"source": "src/tdp-day5.md", "kind": "Day 5"}, "file": "d5-day5.md", "notes": ["<p>original title (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"Day 5 — Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Day overview:</strong> Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 4, then walks through Rod 6 (Renewing) and its eight practical sub-steps in depth. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 7 (Rebuilding) and the Gates of Jerusalem allegory, followed by the week's closing session — testimonies, re-scoring/reflecting on the questionnaire, and commissioning. This is the final day of the whole program.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## PART 1 — Renewing the Mind\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 4 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 7 of 10) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- One-slide reminder of Day 4: the Altar and Temple; Rod 1 Revelation, Rod 2 Repentance, Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce; last night's Personal Ministry Time\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Today's turn: \"today we finish building the Wall of Salvation — starting with how the mind actually gets renewed\"\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18; was Day 4): \"### Slide 3 — The Altar and the Temple\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 2 — Rod 6: Renewing\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 3 — The Eight Steps — Overview\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 4 — Step 1–2: Acknowledging and Believing\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Acknowledging: naming the old belief/thought pattern honestly (connects back to Day 4's Revelation)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 5 — Step 3–4: Confessing and Desiring\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 6 — Step 5: Exercising (the A-C-D-B Model)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 7 — [exercise] Practice the A-C-D-B Loop — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 8 — Step 6–8: Forgiving, Growing, Helping\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 9 — [story about xxx — an example of someone (could be yourself) whose own healing overflowed into helping someone else walk the same path]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 10 — What's Coming in Part 2\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the final rod — Rebuilding — and the Gates of Jerusalem, followed by the week's closing session\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"## PART 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Commissioning\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"<strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 16, the 6th of 11 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the entire week's recap (not just today's) and dedicated time for encouragements, not just questions. This is the final session of the whole program.\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 11 — Opening: What's Next\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 12 — Rod 7: Rebuilding\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 13 — The Gates of Jerusalem — Introduction\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 14 — The Gates in Depth (Part 1)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 15 — The Gates in Depth (Part 2)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 16 — [exercise] Match the Gate to Its Meaning — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 17 — The Gates in Depth (Part 3)\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 18 — [story about xxx — an image or moment of your own \"rebuilding\" becoming visible to others, something concrete that changed once the inner wall was restored]\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 19 — The Character of the King of Glory\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Invite a quiet, optional reflection (not a break activity): look back at your Day 2 questionnaire results — not a formal re-take, just noticing what feels different after one week; one week won't erase a lifetime pattern, but naming a starting point matters\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 20 — Recap: The Whole Week + Time for Encouragements\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline → behavior built like a wall → the walls and situations → behind the walls → restoration through the seven rods → rebuilding and the gates\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Q3, 2026-11-16): \"- Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline → behavior built like a wall → the walls and profiles → behind the walls → restoration through the seven rods → rebuilding and the gates\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"### Slide 21 — Closing and Commissioning\"</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Echo Day 4's images one last time: God is still refining as gold and shaping as clay — He doesn't leave anyone to themselves, and the work continues long after this week ends\"</p>\n", "<p>added (Restructure, 2026-11-18)</p>\n", "<p>original (Restructure, 2026-11-18): \"- Close in prayer — a commissioning prayer/blessing over the group as they carry this into their DTS outreach and beyond\"</p>\n"], "blocks": [{"ref": "D.5 §0 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-0-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2c6c999c08", "text": "Day 5 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning"}, {"ref": "D.5 §0 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-0-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "1b0fe95784", "text": "Day overview: The landing of the week. Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 4, introduces the daily cross — the altar fulfilled, the one sacrifice we keep coming back to — with the Altar and the Temple (Ezra 3), then walks through Rod 6 (Renewing) and its eight practical sub-steps in depth. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 7 (Rebuilding) and the Gates of Jerusalem allegory, followed by the week's closing session — testimonies, re-scoring/reflecting on the questionnaire, and recommissioning. This is the final day of the whole program."}, {"ref": "D.5 §1 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-1-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "9a5dc50b53", "text": "PART 1 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind"}, {"ref": "D.5 §1 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-1-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "45dc900fcb", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with the Day 4 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 9 of 12) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break."}, {"ref": "D.5 §2 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-2-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2aeec36b3e", "text": "Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions"}, {"ref": "D.5 §2 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-2-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9a73276c39", "text": "Open in prayer One-slide reminder of Day 4: the Wall of Rejection, the Lust Trap/Flight Swing and the remedy of God-declared worth; the Final Swing; Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce; this week's one-on-one times Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday Today's turn: \"today we land — at the daily cross — and finish building the Wall of Salvation, starting with how the mind actually gets renewed\""}, {"ref": "D.5 §3 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-3-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "508342418d", "text": "Slide 2 — The Daily Cross"}, {"ref": "D.5 §3 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-3-1", "kind": "list", "h": "3bb458fc99", "text": "The daily cross: the one sacrifice already made — Christ's — that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles Not a new sacrifice every time, but a return to the finished one: what we bring is the brick, the lie, the offense; what we receive is forgiveness and a fresh start The link with Rod 3: the book's study guide calls the Release/Forgiveness session \"The Daily Cross\" — receiving forgiveness and releasing others is what we bring here, every day Not only for this week: this is the place to come back to after the one-on-ones, after outreach, after every fall"}, {"ref": "D.5 §4 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-4-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "fb41aee2f7", "text": "Slide 3 — The Altar and the Temple"}, {"ref": "D.5 §4 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-4-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9292f50464", "text": "Introduce the book's imagery: before rebuilding walls, Scripture's pattern is to rebuild the altar and the temple first — worship and God's presence come before structural repair The Altar (Ezra 3): the place you return to every time you sin — confession, repentance, forgiveness The daily cross is the altar fulfilled: the one sacrifice already made, that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles The Temple: the ongoing life with God, lived from there — worship, thanksgiving, prayer, His presence Point: restoration starts with reorienting toward God, not just fixing behavior"}, {"ref": "D.5 §5 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-5-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d9a1c22505", "text": "Slide 4 — Rod 6: Renewing"}, {"ref": "D.5 §5 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-5-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f38bac72cb", "text": "Define renewing the mind (Romans 12:2) — not a one-time event, but an ongoing replacement of old thought patterns with truth Position this as the longest and most practical of the seven rods — eight concrete sub-steps Renewing is what the life in the Temple looks like day to day, lived from the altar"}, {"ref": "D.5 §6 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-6-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "743e7f0c35", "text": "Slide 5 — The Eight Steps — Overview"}, {"ref": "D.5 §6 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-6-1", "kind": "list", "h": "c7dee3ea2b", "text": "List all eight sub-steps up front so the room can see the shape of the whole process: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping Note: some of these echo earlier rods (like forgiving) — renewing weaves them into an ongoing daily practice, not just a one-time crisis response"}, {"ref": "D.5 §7 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-7-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "78f58bfe3b", "text": "Slide 6 — Step 1–2: Acknowledging and Believing"}, {"ref": "D.5 §7 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-7-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6ef1aa8bac", "text": "Acknowledging: naming the old belief/thought pattern honestly (connects back to Wednesday's Revelation) Believing: actively choosing to trust what God says over what the old pattern says [story about xxx — a specific old belief about yourself you had to consciously replace with what God says, and what that process actually looked like day to day]"}, {"ref": "D.5 §8 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-8-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "09b41516d4", "text": "Slide 7 — Step 3–4: Confessing and Desiring"}, {"ref": "D.5 §8 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-8-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6d298cde5a", "text": "Confessing: speaking the truth out loud, not just thinking it — reinforces the new pattern through voice and hearing Desiring: cultivating an actual want for the change, not just a grudging obligation to change Quick written exercise: writing one \"confession\" statement replacing an old belief with a biblical truth"}, {"ref": "D.5 §9 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-9-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "99dae237a5", "text": "Slide 8 — Step 5: Exercising (the A-C-D-B Model)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §9 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-9-1", "kind": "list", "h": "565d8a02c7", "text": "Walk through the A-C-D-B model as the book presents it (Acknowledge, Confess, Desire, Believe, in practiced repetition) — a mini-loop for daily practice Frame as a spiritual \"muscle memory\" exercise — repetition over time, not instant transformation"}, {"ref": "D.5 §10 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-10-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "e849461c9a", "text": "Slide 9 — [exercise] Practice the A-C-D-B Loop — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.5 §10 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-10-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4daa6f661c", "text": "Walk the whole room through one full round of the A-C-D-B loop together, using a shared, low-stakes example truth (e.g., replacing \"I have to earn love\" with \"I am already loved\") Then invite people to privately run the loop once more with their own specific truth from earlier in the week Good active, participatory break after the halfway point of the session"}, {"ref": "D.5 §11 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-11-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "acafeea2bd", "text": "Slide 10 — Step 6–8: Forgiving, Growing, Helping"}, {"ref": "D.5 §11 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-11-1", "kind": "list", "h": "6f372de9f3", "text": "Forgiving: an ongoing posture, not a one-time event (ties back to Rod 3) Growing: renewing is a lifelong process, not a finish line reached this week Helping: healed people help others — turning personal restoration outward toward serving others"}, {"ref": "D.5 §12 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-12-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "286ab73868", "text": "Slide 11 — [story about xxx — an example of someone (could be yourself) whose own healing overflowed into helping someone else walk the same path]"}, {"ref": "D.5 §13 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-13-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "472eebbf19", "text": "Slide 12 — What's Coming in Part 2"}, {"ref": "D.5 §13 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-13-1", "kind": "list", "h": "9ffed2b644", "text": "Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the final rod — Rebuilding — and the Gates of Jerusalem, followed by the week's closing session and recommissioning Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after Close in prayer"}, {"ref": "D.5 §14 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-14-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2d3d6b4775", "text": "☕ SHORT BREAK"}, {"ref": "D.5 §15 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-15-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "0818a757e8", "text": "PART 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning"}, {"ref": "D.5 §15 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-15-1", "kind": "paragraph", "h": "4407b47efb", "text": "Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs): opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 18, the 6th of 11 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the entire week's recap (not just today's) and dedicated time for encouragements, not just questions. This is the final session of the whole program."}, {"ref": "D.5 §16 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-16-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "51f6353cff", "text": "Slide 13 — Opening: What's Next"}, {"ref": "D.5 §16 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-16-1", "kind": "list", "h": "d2fa9d818f", "text": "Open in prayer Quick, light mention only: \"we've renewed the mind — now, in our last session together, we finish the wall itself\" If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on"}, {"ref": "D.5 §17 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-17-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5e47846337", "text": "Slide 14 — Rod 7: Rebuilding"}, {"ref": "D.5 §17 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-17-1", "kind": "list", "h": "67d1f8ff26", "text": "Define rebuilding as the practical, ongoing construction of a new, healthy wall — bricks now laid on the true plumbline (Christ) instead of the false prophets from Day 1 Point back to Nehemiah's rebuilding of Jerusalem's actual walls as the scriptural backbone for this final rod The same order as this morning: Ezra rebuilt the altar and the temple first; Nehemiah then rebuilt the walls"}, {"ref": "D.5 §18 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-18-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "a0c12bca1f", "text": "Slide 15 — The Gates of Jerusalem — Introduction"}, {"ref": "D.5 §18 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-18-1", "kind": "list", "h": "1f69980c22", "text": "Introduce Nehemiah's ten gates as an allegory for different aspects of restored life Brief list of the gates: Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch"}, {"ref": "D.5 §19 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-19-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "dd58fe64f4", "text": "Slide 16 — The Gates in Depth (Part 1)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §19 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-19-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4d84400249", "text": "Sheep Gate (sacrifice/relationship with God), Fish Gate (evangelism/mission), Old Gate (tradition/heritage), Valley Gate (humility) Brief meaning for each, tied back to practical daily life"}, {"ref": "D.5 §20 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-20-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "7036ce00c9", "text": "Slide 17 — The Gates in Depth (Part 2)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §20 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-20-1", "kind": "list", "h": "f87bff46b3", "text": "Rubbish Gate (dealing with what needs to be discarded), Fountain Gate (the Holy Spirit/refreshing), Water Gate (the Word of God), Horse Gate (spiritual warfare/strength) Continue brief meanings tied to practical life"}, {"ref": "D.5 §21 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-21-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "5fcfa30153", "text": "Slide 18 — [exercise] Match the Gate to Its Meaning — MIDPOINT BREAK & REFOCUS"}, {"ref": "D.5 §21 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-21-1", "kind": "list", "h": "daf5253b19", "text": "Quick, energetic group activity: shuffle cards or slips with gate names and meanings, have teams race to match them correctly Good active break at the halfway point of this final session, before moving into the closing material"}, {"ref": "D.5 §22 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-22-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "30bdd7ae4d", "text": "Slide 19 — The Gates in Depth (Part 3)"}, {"ref": "D.5 §22 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-22-1", "kind": "list", "h": "4e06cf7fe2", "text": "East Gate (the Lord's return/hope), Watch Gate (vigilance/perseverance) Close the allegory: a rebuilt wall isn't just repaired — it's a whole functioning city again, open to the right traffic and guarded against the wrong"}, {"ref": "D.5 §23 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-23-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "c595cb087f", "text": "Slide 20 — [story about xxx — an image or moment of your own \"rebuilding\" becoming visible to others, something concrete that changed once the inner wall was restored]"}, {"ref": "D.5 §24 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-24-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "d02f3f1eeb", "text": "Slide 21 — The Character of the King of Glory"}, {"ref": "D.5 §24 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-24-1", "kind": "list", "h": "8b68571435", "text": "Close the teaching portion of the week with Psalm 24's \"King of Glory\" image — the one the gates are ultimately opened for Tie back to the entire week's throughline: from a crooked wall measured against a false plumbline, to a rebuilt wall measured against Christ Invite a quiet, optional reflection (not a break activity): look back at the questionnaire you filled in on Monday night — not a formal re-take, just noticing what feels different after one week; one week won't erase a lifetime pattern, but naming a starting point matters"}, {"ref": "D.5 §25 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-25-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "809dea6834", "text": "Slide 22 — Recap: The Whole Week + Time for Encouragements"}, {"ref": "D.5 §25 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-25-1", "kind": "list", "h": "5f4d59e709", "text": "Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline and the seven false prophets → behavior built like a wall → the questionnaire → the two walls and the four typical situations → Rebellion and Rejection in depth, with the one-on-one times → restoration through the seven rods → the daily cross, the altar and the temple → rebuilding and the gates This is the final session, so give this its own real time — not just a question slot: invite testimonies and words of encouragement for one another, not only questions Optional: invite one or two volunteers to briefly share what stood out to them this week"}, {"ref": "D.5 §26 ¶0", "anchor": "p-D.5-26-0", "kind": "heading", "h": "2b7db0787b", "text": "Slide 23 — Closing and Recommissioning"}, {"ref": "D.5 §26 ¶1", "anchor": "p-D.5-26-1", "kind": "list", "h": "46c797d58a", "text": "Final encouraging word: this week is a beginning, not a finish line Echo Wednesday's images one last time: God is still refining as gold and shaping as clay — He doesn't leave anyone to themselves, and the work continues long after this week ends Send them out with the daily cross: the altar they can come back to every day, long after this week [story about xxx — a closing word of encouragement drawn from your own long-term experience of walking out this material, to send the group off well] Close in prayer — a recommissioning prayer/blessing over the group as they carry this into their DTS outreach and beyond"}], "html": "<div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2c6c999c08\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §0 ¶0\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.5-0-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §0 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §0 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶0</button><h1 data-body=\"1\">Day 5 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind / Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning</h1></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1b0fe95784\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §0 ¶1\" data-sec=\"0\" id=\"p-D.5-0-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §0 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §0 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§0 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Day overview:</strong> The landing of the week. Morning session (Part 1) opens with a recap of Day 4, introduces the daily cross — the altar fulfilled, the one sacrifice we keep coming back to — with the Altar and the Temple (Ezra 3), then walks through Rod 6 (Renewing) and its eight practical sub-steps in depth. Afternoon session (Part 2) covers Rod 7 (Rebuilding) and the Gates of Jerusalem allegory, followed by the week's closing session — testimonies, re-scoring/reflecting on the questionnaire, and recommissioning. This is the final day of the whole program.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9a5dc50b53\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §1 ¶0\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.5-1-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §1 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §1 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 1 — The Daily Cross and Renewing the Mind</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"45dc900fcb\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §1 ¶1\" data-sec=\"1\" id=\"p-D.5-1-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §1 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §1 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§1 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with the Day 4 recap and takes any questions that came up during the day. One exercise this session, positioned after the halfway point (Slide 9 of 12) as a physical interruption to refocus. Closes with just a quick mention of what Part 2 covers — no full recap yet, questions held for the break.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2aeec36b3e\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §2 ¶0\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.5-2-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §2 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §2 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 1 — Yesterday, Quickly — and Any Questions</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9a73276c39\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §2 ¶1\" data-sec=\"2\" id=\"p-D.5-2-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §2 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §2 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§2 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>One-slide reminder of Day 4: the Wall of Rejection, the Lust Trap/Flight Swing and the remedy of God-declared worth; the Final Swing; Rod 3 Release/Forgiveness, Rods 4–5 Recognize/Renounce; this week's one-on-one times</li>\n<li>Open the floor briefly for any questions that came up for people since yesterday</li>\n<li>Today's turn: \"today we land — at the daily cross — and finish building the Wall of Salvation, starting with how the mind actually gets renewed\"</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"508342418d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §3 ¶0\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.5-3-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §3 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §3 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 2 — The Daily Cross</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"3bb458fc99\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §3 ¶1\" data-sec=\"3\" id=\"p-D.5-3-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §3 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §3 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§3 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>The daily cross: the one sacrifice already made — Christ's — that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles</li>\n<li>Not a new sacrifice every time, but a return to the finished one: what we bring is the brick, the lie, the offense; what we receive is forgiveness and a fresh start</li>\n<li>The link with Rod 3: the book's study guide calls the Release/Forgiveness session \"The Daily Cross\" — receiving forgiveness and releasing others is what we bring here, every day</li>\n<li>Not only for this week: this is the place to come back to after the one-on-ones, after outreach, after every fall</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"fb41aee2f7\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §4 ¶0\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.5-4-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §4 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §4 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 3 — The Altar and the Temple</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9292f50464\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §4 ¶1\" data-sec=\"4\" id=\"p-D.5-4-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §4 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §4 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§4 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce the book's imagery: before rebuilding walls, Scripture's pattern is to rebuild the altar and the temple first — worship and God's presence come before structural repair</li>\n<li>The Altar (Ezra 3): the place you return to every time you sin — confession, repentance, forgiveness</li>\n<li>The daily cross is the altar fulfilled: the one sacrifice already made, that we keep coming back to with our daily struggles</li>\n<li>The Temple: the ongoing life with God, lived from there — worship, thanksgiving, prayer, His presence</li>\n<li>Point: restoration starts with reorienting toward God, not just fixing behavior</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d9a1c22505\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §5 ¶0\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.5-5-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §5 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §5 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 4 — Rod 6: Renewing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f38bac72cb\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §5 ¶1\" data-sec=\"5\" id=\"p-D.5-5-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §5 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §5 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§5 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define renewing the mind (Romans 12:2) — not a one-time event, but an ongoing replacement of old thought patterns with truth</li>\n<li>Position this as the longest and most practical of the seven rods — eight concrete sub-steps</li>\n<li>Renewing is what the life in the Temple looks like day to day, lived from the altar</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"743e7f0c35\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §6 ¶0\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.5-6-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §6 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §6 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 5 — The Eight Steps — Overview</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c7dee3ea2b\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §6 ¶1\" data-sec=\"6\" id=\"p-D.5-6-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §6 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §6 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§6 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>List all eight sub-steps up front so the room can see the shape of the whole process: Acknowledging, Believing, Confessing, Desiring, Exercising (the A-C-D-B model), Forgiving, Growing, Helping</li>\n<li>Note: some of these echo earlier rods (like forgiving) — renewing weaves them into an ongoing daily practice, not just a one-time crisis response</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"78f58bfe3b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §7 ¶0\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.5-7-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §7 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §7 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 6 — Step 1–2: Acknowledging and Believing</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6ef1aa8bac\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §7 ¶1\" data-sec=\"7\" id=\"p-D.5-7-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §7 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §7 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§7 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Acknowledging: naming the old belief/thought pattern honestly (connects back to Wednesday's Revelation)</li>\n<li>Believing: actively choosing to trust what God says over what the old pattern says</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a specific old belief about yourself you had to consciously replace with what God says, and what that process actually looked like day to day]</span></li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"09b41516d4\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §8 ¶0\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.5-8-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §8 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §8 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 7 — Step 3–4: Confessing and Desiring</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6d298cde5a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §8 ¶1\" data-sec=\"8\" id=\"p-D.5-8-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §8 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §8 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§8 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Confessing: speaking the truth out loud, not just thinking it — reinforces the new pattern through voice and hearing</li>\n<li>Desiring: cultivating an actual want for the change, not just a grudging obligation to change</li>\n<li>Quick written exercise: writing one \"confession\" statement replacing an old belief with a biblical truth</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"99dae237a5\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §9 ¶0\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.5-9-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §9 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §9 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 8 — Step 5: Exercising (the A-C-D-B Model)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"565d8a02c7\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §9 ¶1\" data-sec=\"9\" id=\"p-D.5-9-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §9 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §9 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§9 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Walk through the A-C-D-B model as the book presents it (Acknowledge, Confess, Desire, Believe, in practiced repetition) — a mini-loop for daily practice</li>\n<li>Frame as a spiritual \"muscle memory\" exercise — repetition over time, not instant transformation</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"e849461c9a\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §10 ¶0\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.5-10-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §10 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §10 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 9 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Practice the A-C-D-B Loop — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4daa6f661c\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §10 ¶1\" data-sec=\"10\" id=\"p-D.5-10-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §10 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §10 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§10 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Walk the whole room through one full round of the A-C-D-B loop together, using a shared, low-stakes example truth (e.g., replacing \"I have to earn love\" with \"I am already loved\")</li>\n<li>Then invite people to privately run the loop once more with their own specific truth from earlier in the week</li>\n<li>Good active, participatory break after the halfway point of the session</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"acafeea2bd\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §11 ¶0\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.5-11-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §11 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §11 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 10 — Step 6–8: Forgiving, Growing, Helping</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"6f372de9f3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §11 ¶1\" data-sec=\"11\" id=\"p-D.5-11-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §11 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §11 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§11 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Forgiving: an ongoing posture, not a one-time event (ties back to Rod 3)</li>\n<li>Growing: renewing is a lifelong process, not a finish line reached this week</li>\n<li>Helping: healed people help others — turning personal restoration outward toward serving others</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"286ab73868\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §12 ¶0\" data-sec=\"12\" id=\"p-D.5-12-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §12 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §12 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§12 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 11 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — an example of someone (could be yourself) whose own healing overflowed into helping someone else walk the same path]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"472eebbf19\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §13 ¶0\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.5-13-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §13 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §13 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 12 — What's Coming in Part 2</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"9ffed2b644\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §13 ¶1\" data-sec=\"13\" id=\"p-D.5-13-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §13 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §13 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§13 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, light mention only — no recap yet: after the break, the final rod — Rebuilding — and the Gates of Jerusalem, followed by the week's closing session and recommissioning</li>\n<li>Hold questions for the break — we'll pick up discussion after</li>\n<li>Close in prayer</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2d3d6b4775\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §14 ¶0\" data-sec=\"14\" id=\"p-D.5-14-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §14 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §14 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§14 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">☕ SHORT BREAK</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"0818a757e8\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §15 ¶0\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.5-15-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §15 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §15 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶0</button><h2 data-body=\"1\">PART 2 — Rebuilding, the Gates of Jerusalem, and Recommissioning</h2></div><div class=\"blk blk-paragraph\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4407b47efb\" data-kind=\"paragraph\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §15 ¶1\" data-sec=\"15\" id=\"p-D.5-15-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §15 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §15 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§15 ¶1</button><p data-body=\"1\"><strong>Pacing note (≈1.5 hrs):</strong> opens with just a quick mention of what's ahead (and a chance to answer any plenum question from the break) — no recap needed yet. One exercise this session, positioned at the halfway point (Slide 18, the 6th of 11 slides in this Part) as a physical interruption to refocus — not earlier. Closes with the entire week's recap (not just today's) and dedicated time for encouragements, not just questions. This is the final session of the whole program.</p></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"51f6353cff\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §16 ¶0\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.5-16-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §16 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §16 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 13 — Opening: What's Next</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d2fa9d818f\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §16 ¶1\" data-sec=\"16\" id=\"p-D.5-16-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §16 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §16 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§16 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Open in prayer</li>\n<li>Quick, light mention only: \"we've renewed the mind — now, in our last session together, we finish the wall itself\"</li>\n<li>If a question came up in the break, take a moment to answer it in plenum here before moving on</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5e47846337\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §17 ¶0\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.5-17-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §17 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §17 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 14 — Rod 7: Rebuilding</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"67d1f8ff26\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §17 ¶1\" data-sec=\"17\" id=\"p-D.5-17-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §17 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §17 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§17 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Define rebuilding as the practical, ongoing construction of a new, healthy wall — bricks now laid on the true plumbline (Christ) instead of the false prophets from Day 1</li>\n<li>Point back to Nehemiah's rebuilding of Jerusalem's actual walls as the scriptural backbone for this final rod</li>\n<li>The same order as this morning: Ezra rebuilt the altar and the temple first; Nehemiah then rebuilt the walls</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"a0c12bca1f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §18 ¶0\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.5-18-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §18 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §18 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 15 — The Gates of Jerusalem — Introduction</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"1f69980c22\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §18 ¶1\" data-sec=\"18\" id=\"p-D.5-18-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §18 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §18 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§18 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Introduce Nehemiah's ten gates as an allegory for different aspects of restored life</li>\n<li>Brief list of the gates: Sheep, Fish, Old, Valley, Rubbish, Fountain, Water, Horse, East, Watch</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"dd58fe64f4\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §19 ¶0\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.5-19-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §19 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §19 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 16 — The Gates in Depth (Part 1)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4d84400249\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §19 ¶1\" data-sec=\"19\" id=\"p-D.5-19-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §19 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §19 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§19 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Sheep Gate (sacrifice/relationship with God), Fish Gate (evangelism/mission), Old Gate (tradition/heritage), Valley Gate (humility)</li>\n<li>Brief meaning for each, tied back to practical daily life</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"7036ce00c9\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §20 ¶0\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.5-20-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §20 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §20 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 17 — The Gates in Depth (Part 2)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"f87bff46b3\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §20 ¶1\" data-sec=\"20\" id=\"p-D.5-20-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §20 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §20 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§20 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Rubbish Gate (dealing with what needs to be discarded), Fountain Gate (the Holy Spirit/refreshing), Water Gate (the Word of God), Horse Gate (spiritual warfare/strength)</li>\n<li>Continue brief meanings tied to practical life</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5fcfa30153\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §21 ¶0\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.5-21-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §21 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §21 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 18 — <span class=\"ph-exercise\">[exercise]</span> Match the Gate to Its Meaning — MIDPOINT BREAK &amp; REFOCUS</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"daf5253b19\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §21 ¶1\" data-sec=\"21\" id=\"p-D.5-21-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §21 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §21 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§21 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Quick, energetic group activity: shuffle cards or slips with gate names and meanings, have teams race to match them correctly</li>\n<li>Good active break at the halfway point of this final session, before moving into the closing material</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"30bdd7ae4d\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §22 ¶0\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.5-22-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §22 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §22 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 19 — The Gates in Depth (Part 3)</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"4e06cf7fe2\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §22 ¶1\" data-sec=\"22\" id=\"p-D.5-22-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §22 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §22 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§22 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>East Gate (the Lord's return/hope), Watch Gate (vigilance/perseverance)</li>\n<li>Close the allegory: a rebuilt wall isn't just repaired — it's a whole functioning city again, open to the right traffic and guarded against the wrong</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"c595cb087f\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §23 ¶0\" data-sec=\"23\" id=\"p-D.5-23-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §23 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §23 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§23 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 20 — <span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — an image or moment of your own \"rebuilding\" becoming visible to others, something concrete that changed once the inner wall was restored]</span></h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"d02f3f1eeb\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §24 ¶0\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.5-24-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §24 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §24 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 21 — The Character of the King of Glory</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"8b68571435\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §24 ¶1\" data-sec=\"24\" id=\"p-D.5-24-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §24 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §24 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§24 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Close the teaching portion of the week with Psalm 24's \"King of Glory\" image — the one the gates are ultimately opened for</li>\n<li>Tie back to the entire week's throughline: from a crooked wall measured against a false plumbline, to a rebuilt wall measured against Christ</li>\n<li>Invite a quiet, optional reflection (not a break activity): look back at the questionnaire you filled in on Monday night — not a formal re-take, just noticing what feels different after one week; one week won't erase a lifetime pattern, but naming a starting point matters</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"809dea6834\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §25 ¶0\" data-sec=\"25\" id=\"p-D.5-25-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §25 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §25 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§25 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 22 — Recap: The Whole Week + Time for Encouragements</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"5f4d59e709\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §25 ¶1\" data-sec=\"25\" id=\"p-D.5-25-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §25 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §25 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§25 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Full recap of the entire week, not just today: the plumbline and the seven false prophets → behavior built like a wall → the questionnaire → the two walls and the four typical situations → Rebellion and Rejection in depth, with the one-on-one times → restoration through the seven rods → the daily cross, the altar and the temple → rebuilding and the gates</li>\n<li>This is the final session, so give this its own real time — not just a question slot: invite testimonies and words of encouragement for one another, not only questions</li>\n<li>Optional: invite one or two volunteers to briefly share what stood out to them this week</li>\n</ul></div><div class=\"blk blk-heading\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"2b7db0787b\" data-kind=\"heading\" data-par=\"0\" data-ref=\"D.5 §26 ¶0\" data-sec=\"26\" id=\"p-D.5-26-0\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §26 ¶0\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §26 ¶0 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§26 ¶0</button><h3 data-body=\"1\">Slide 23 — Closing and Recommissioning</h3></div><div class=\"blk blk-list\" data-editable=\"1\" data-h=\"46c797d58a\" data-kind=\"list\" data-par=\"1\" data-ref=\"D.5 §26 ¶1\" data-sec=\"26\" id=\"p-D.5-26-1\"><button aria-label=\"Reference D.5 §26 ¶1\" class=\"ref\" title=\"D.5 §26 ¶1 (click to copy the reference and show actions)\" type=\"button\">§26 ¶1</button><ul data-body=\"1\">\n<li>Final encouraging word: this week is a beginning, not a finish line</li>\n<li>Echo Wednesday's images one last time: God is still refining as gold and shaping as clay — He doesn't leave anyone to themselves, and the work continues long after this week ends</li>\n<li>Send them out with the daily cross: the altar they can come back to every day, long after this week</li>\n<li><span class=\"ph-story\">[story about xxx — a closing word of encouragement drawn from your own long-term experience of walking out this material, to send the group off well]</span></li>\n<li>Close in prayer — a recommissioning prayer/blessing over the group as they carry this into their DTS outreach and beyond</li>\n</ul></div>"}], "pages": {"help": "<h1>Help</h1>\n<p>This reader shows <strong>The Divine Plumbline</strong> teaching material: an overview, the 5-day plan and the five day texts. You can read it, change it in place, propose removals and leave comments. Your feedback stays in this browser until you export it.</p>\n<h2>Parts and references</h2>\n<table class=\"table table-sm table-bordered\">\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>O.1</strong></td>\n<td>Overview: a summary of the week, two sections per day (the reader opens here)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>P.1</strong></td>\n<td>The 5-day DTS program (plan for the week)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D.1 – D.5</strong></td>\n<td>Day 1 to Day 5: slide-by-slide teaching text</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Every block has a reference in the margin, like <strong>§3 ¶4</strong>. The full reference is <code>PART §section ¶paragraph</code>, for example <code>D.2 §3 ¶4</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>§0</strong> is the part title and the text before the first heading (the day overview).</li>\n<li>Every heading starts a new section and is <strong>¶0</strong> of it: <em>PART 1</em>, each <em>Slide</em>, <em>☕ SHORT BREAK</em>, <em>PART 2</em>; in the plan each <em>DAY</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>¶</strong> counts the blocks after the heading: usually ¶1 is the bullet list of a slide, ¶2 a delivery note under it.</li>\n<li>Section numbers are not slide numbers. In Day 1, <em>Slide 1</em> is §2 because <em>PART 1</em> is §1. The § ¶ badge in the margin always shows the right one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Click a reference to copy it and to show the action buttons (useful on a phone). In chat you can write for example: <em>\"D.1 §5 ¶1: shorten this list\"</em> (the bullet list of Slide 4).</p>\n<h2>Reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Parts</strong> on the left (the ☰ button on a phone). Previous / next links are at the bottom of each part.</li>\n<li><strong>Dark / light</strong>: the moon / sun button. The choice is remembered in this browser.</li>\n<li>Highlighted in yellow: <strong>[story about xxx …]</strong> placeholders, where the presenter tells a story of their own. Green: <strong>[exercise]</strong> tags.</li>\n<li>On a phone, the <strong>sliders</strong> button at the top right shows the title, tabs and the editing switch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Editing a block</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Click a paragraph, heading or list (or the ✎ button). It turns yellow.</li>\n<li>Type your change. <strong>Enter</strong> saves (in lists Enter starts a new item; use <strong>Ctrl+Enter</strong> to save). <strong>Shift+Enter</strong> makes a line break. <strong>Esc</strong> cancels.</li>\n<li><strong>Save</strong>, <strong>Cancel</strong> or <strong>Reset to source</strong>. An edited block is marked <em>Edited</em>; <em>Show original</em> lets you compare.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Only one block is edited at a time. Turn <strong>Editing off</strong> in the top bar to read without accidental edits. Links still work with a normal click.</p>\n<p>If the source text changes after you edited it (a new build), the block shows <strong>Source changed since…</strong> and your edit is not applied. You can <em>Edit again</em> or <em>Discard</em>.</p>\n<h2>Removing and commenting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>🗑 <strong>Propose removal</strong>: the block is struck through. <strong>Restore</strong> undoes it. The original text is kept for the export.</li>\n<li>💬 <strong>Comment</strong>: write a note; it appears under the block with a blue bar.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>My feedback and export</h2>\n<p>The <strong>My feedback</strong> tab lists all your changes, removals and comments by part, with links back to the text. Each item has <strong>Undo</strong> / <strong>Delete</strong>. <strong>Clear all</strong> asks first.</p>\n<p>Export with <strong>Copy to clipboard</strong>, <strong>Download .md</strong> or <strong>Download .json</strong>, and paste or send it back in the chat. The Markdown looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code># Feedback on The Divine Plumbline\nExported 2026-11-15 14:05 · reader built 2026-11-15 10:00 · reader v1.5.0\nContent: 1 changes, 1 removed, 1 comments\n## D.1 Day 1 — Welcome &amp; The Plumbline / Truth and Deception\n### D.1 §5 ¶1 — CHANGED\nBefore: \"old text\"\nAfter: \"new text\"\n### D.1 §6 ¶1 — REMOVED\nBefore: \"text\"\n- D.1 §14 ¶1 — COMMENT: my comment\n  &gt; «start of the block»\n</code></pre>\n<p>A list edit is exported as the whole list, Before and After.</p>\n<h2>Editor notes (for authors)</h2>\n<p>An HTML comment <code>&lt;!-- … --&gt;</code> or a fenced block with the language <code>EDITOR-NOTE</code> is hidden from the text and shown in an <strong>Editor notes</strong> panel next to it.</p>\n<h2>Privacy</h2>\n<p>Nothing is sent anywhere. Your feedback is stored in this browser's local storage. Clearing browser data deletes it, so export first. The link is unlisted but not secret: anyone who has it can read this copy.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p>reader v1.5.0 · built 2026-11-18 20:18</p>\n"}, "stats": {"parts": 7, "blocks": 287, "notes": 210, "links_internal": 5, "links_external": 0, "placeholders": 23}};
